{"https://villumfonden.dk/en/group/channel/enabling-capable-citizens-2027": {"text": "Enabling Capable Citizens 2027 Enabling young people to become capable citizens in the green transition and encouraging their motivation for education Apply The Enabling Capable Citizens (ECC) programme seeks to empower young people, particularly at-risk youth, to become capable citizens in European societies undergoing the green transition. By green transition, we understand a systemic transformation towards sustainability across all sectors of society, requiring new approaches to education, skills and participation for all young people. In ECC, focus is on providing equal opportunities for at-risk children and young people, ensuring that they are not left behind in the green transition. In this context, at risk refers to children and young people with immigrant or refugee backgrounds, as well as those from socially, geographically or economically disadvantaged circumstances. To support equal opportunities for these groups, we fund initiatives that strengthen their motivation for education and their capacity to understand, engage with and respond to societal challenges. ECC funds long-term projects of three to five years, typically with budgets above EUR 500,000, to create sustainable and lasting impact. In 2027, ECC will support projects based in Poland, Czechia, Slovakia and Hungary. The deadline for the ECC call is 11 January 2027. Two online information meetings will take place. Register for the information meetings below: 1st online information meeting, 3 November 2026: Register here 2nd online information meeting, 9 December 2026: Register here Status Open Full budget of the call EUR 5.3 m Application - Open for submissions 1 September 2026 Application - Deadline for submissions 11 January 2027 14:00 Which activities are funded? Skills needed for the green transition We particularly focus on holistic initiatives that use well-documented methods to enhance children’s and young people’s opportunities to participate as active citizens. Initiatives should improve the educational and employment prospects of at-risk children and young people and enable them to contribute to and benefit from the green transition. Initiatives may be broad in scope or locally anchored and can focus on strengthening students’ green knowledge, establishing renewable energy learning environments , or developing teachers’ competences. We fund activities such as: After-school initiatives offering activities for children and young people to engage with topics related to the green transition. Development of new approaches and teaching methods in schools that foster 21st-century transversal skills such as collaboration, entrepreneurship, problem-solving, and critical thinking. Development and implementation of innovative didactic approaches that utilise digital tools and methods to actively enhance learner engagement and improve learning outcomes. Facilities for new and inspiring learning environments: e.g. makerspaces, workshops, and outdoor learning facilities. Pedagogical approaches and initiatives that address barriers for engaging at-risk children and young people in quality education. Holistic activities in disadvantaged areas aimed at ensuring a smooth transition to education for children and young people. Professional development of teachers to strengthen their competences within the green transition. Enhancing the confidence and skills of children and young people to use digital technologies and foster digital literacy.‌ We do not support: Physical facilities alone, nor do we support property acquisitions or the construction of new properties. Projects that solely or primarily focus on mental health issues or youth with disabilities. Stand-alone activities without developmental perspectives, such as summer camps or running costs alone. Eligibility and prioritisation Who can apply? Schools and other educational institutions, civil society organisations, local innovators and public authorities can apply. Applications from private individuals will not be accepted. Please note that English is our primary language for communicating with applicants and grantees. Applications and appendixes must therefore be submitted in English. Communication with project managers and key personnel must be possible in English without interpreters. Which project setups are supported? We prioritise projects that are: Developmental: Projects should preferably have a clear developmental perspective. Innovative: We support initiatives that introduce new approaches, as well as initiatives that adapt and scale proven solutions which have demonstrated success in achieving long-term sustainable and systemic development in other contexts. Long-term: Project durations spanning three to five years are required. This allows sufficient time to enhance the likelihood of success and achieve systemic impact. Which project costs are eligible? All types of costs can be included in the project budget, provided they are relevant for the project. We strongly encourage ", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-21T14:40:58.413528+00:00"}, "https://villumfonden.dk/en/group/channel/european-vocational-education-and-training-initiative-2027": {"text": "European Vocational Education and Training Initiative 2027 Enabling young people in Europe to become frontrunners in the green transition Apply The European Vocational Education and Training (EVI) programme aims to advance vocational education and training in European societies undergoing the green transition. By green transition, we understand a systemic transformation towards sustainability across all sectors of society. This transition creates a growing need for adapted competences to respond to changing labour market demands across Europe and requires us to rethink how we approach jobs, skills and education. The programme supports initiatives that strengthen green skills, prepare students for future-oriented jobs and enhance the quality and attractiveness of VET through skilled teachers, modern learning environments and diverse student participation. EVI funds long-term projects of three to five years, typically with budgets above EUR 500,000, to create sustainable and lasting impact. In 2027, EVI will support projects based in Poland, Czechia, Slovakia and Hungary. The deadline for the EVI call is 20 May 2027. Three online information meetings will take place. Register for the information meetings below: 1st online information meeting, 3 December 2026: Register here 2nd online information meeting, 28 January 2027: Register here 3rd online information meeting, 1 April 2027: Register here Status Open Full budget of the call EUR 12 m Application - Open for submissions 1 September 2026 Application - Deadline for submissions 20 May 2027 14:00 Which activities are funded? VET skills needed for the green transition We welcome approaches that incorporate student-centred pedagogy, problem-based methods and dual training, while also including transversal skills such as collaboration, problem-solving and entrepreneurship with the aim of contributing to the green transition. We also fund the professional development and further education of teachers and school leaders in relation to the green transition. We expect technical training and professional development to be a significant part of the project, and commitment and participation from management are imperative. A thriving study environment that welcomes students/apprentices and creates strong learning communities is a crucial feature of high-quality VET institutions. We encourage applicants to integrate inclusive practices and to implement recruitment and retention measures as part of the project. We fund activities such as: Professional development of teachers to strengthen their competences, including technical skills, within the green transition. Development and implementation of innovative didactic approaches that utilise digital tools and methods to actively enhance learner engagement and improve learning outcomes. Strengthening entrepreneurship, apprenticeships and pathways from education to green jobs through partnerships between education providers and local employers. Integration of sustainability-related subjects, such as the circular economy, into curricula. Strengthening young people’s digital skills and confidence, including the use of digital technologies and the development of digital literacy. Creation of inclusive study environments that foster strong learning communities and support inclusive practices. Development of new and inspiring learning environments, such as makerspaces, workshops and outdoor learning facilities. Renovation and upgrading of existing buildings to support learning activities and increase the use of renewable energy sources within schools and institutions. We do not support: Physical facilities alone, nor do we support property acquisitions or the construction of new properties. Projects that solely or primarily focus on mental health issues or young people with disabilities. Stand-alone activities without developmental perspectives, such as summer camps or running costs alone. Eligibility and prioritisation Who can apply? Schools and educational institutions, civil society organisations, local innovators and public authorities can apply. Applications from individual persons will not be accepted. Please note that English is our primary language for communicating with applicants and grantees. Applications and appendices must therefore be submitted in English. Communication with project managers and key personnel must be possible in English without interpreters. Which project setups are supported? We prioritise projects that are: Developmental: Projects should preferably have a clear developmental perspective. Innovative: We support initiatives that introduce new approaches, as well as initiatives that adapt and scale proven solutions that have demonstrated success in achieving long-term sustainable and systemic development in other contexts. Long-term: Project durations of three to five years are required. This allows sufficient time to enhance the likelihood of success and achieve systemic impact. Stakeholder-oriented: W", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-21T14:40:59.512966+00:00"}, "https://novonordiskfonden.dk//app/uploads/NNF-Cookie-Policy.pdf": {"text": "%PDF-1.6 %���� 326 0 obj < >stream h�d�AK�@\u0010���ޚE��l�M�\u0014���-\u0014#x�d�vm�#Ӎ��F�=x\u0018x�{�7O��\u0000�^�m O�� Վ��ξm}x�\u0014n��ǓT��u.�K�+?M\u0018F��Dg��A�Ρ�Ŵ�\u0014��L\u0006\"�4��� � `6�\u0012p�0�&�C��7g�B��H,ވ�T�n�Nb���%V��\u000f���R����hȯ�\u0003��\u001b�@6U\u001b1b�k6<$� �\b�T\u0015�ܸt� ���\u0012�\u0010�\u00020\u0005���51���c�2���\b0\u0000��\\� endstream endobj 327 0 obj < >stream h�2�4T0P���w�/�+Q0���L)�6�4\u0005�\u0006)\u0018�I\u00130i\u000e\u00111\u0000Q��!�\u0005��\u0001���vv\u0000\u0001\u0006\u00000�\u0010� endstream endobj 328 0 obj < >stream hޜTmO�0\u0010�+�� 1۱c'\u0012Bj\u0018\u0005$ �t\u0003���\u0015���&Ub4����IڄR\u0004�N����� F\u0019\"�Q�8�G�(��\u0014��Ȟ\u0012�\u0004�\u0011�4\u0004�\u0018Q.\u0019b\u0001ATR��H�\u0004�(�xs\u0006�I�)#8 \u000e\u000e��,ET^k�L�EY-U��F�~#��\\^M\u0010��\b��I�t��ֵ�x�����ٜ�F\u0019�g�ŕ+ wx\b��. ��\u0013|�V�:�/ �T��ڛ�Y\u0010�q�浝\u0000���$I�|�/D�l( v\\H��c���/�*S�W��r F�PNs��v������s�L�>5�6�E;(�n|G�\u0010|fT��F�<׈����/\u0014E~Jp���le� �6��4vc&���2,z\\��Ǭ�㛬\u0018\u0015u���YU����ڡ��ݓ�$�q�\\��鷁F�� \\G��g��l\u001a\u0005�t�]��ڣY����Y\b��Gc \u0018Ɯ\u0010\u0014Z|�\u000f��\u0004 C� Dl�[�yIg��v,�t\"����S��u��Kn_\u0012�\u0010\u0014٢���AG�Q��y���n�FH��� �Z\u0001;�Pq8 ���m �}���|쳕 v@_�`\u0003�D���\u001b�\u000f힃�B���ПfK]� �\u000f]�KU�����\u0012�'Irv��Bl� �J?�\u000ed�:p �����\\ �_�(޵\u0017��h�^� }��_\u0007�\u0006�\u0000Z\u000fK���j��[ �Ѯ@ t\u0010\u0000��\u001b�'\u0014o�\u0014[y3�����P�Ǜq 2'7��?N=��'e��.Z�\u001bh�L|�=���$R�\u0001�\u0006t7�F�(�K���k\u0002m6����d9�u�>4v��\u001aJC�l���&\u0019�f�9[��$�\u000f�����z�)��^\u0004q՝�\u0005�\u0001\u0