. Kontakt [email protected] ved forespørgsler. Se tidligere prismodtagere her . Grant listings All the projects we have funded are listed here. You can customise your search by grant year and category and use free text search. Read more GRAN Managing a grant Read more about how to manage a grant and what general information you need as a grant holder. Read more", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:35.735963+00:00"}, "https://novonordiskfonden.dk/grant/dd2-research-grant-2024/": {"text": "DD2 Research Grant 2024 Call opens 29 August 2024 Call closes 10 December 2024 2:00pm (Copenhagen time) Announcement of results May 2025 Not recurring call Not recurring Application guidelines Rules, grant administration and FAQ About the grants Amount Up to 5 million per grant Location Denmark Committee Komite for Endokrinologi og Metabolisme Contact For grant inquiries Kristina Rohde Larsen Grant Manager, Grant Management [email protected] Scientific inquiries Kate Williams Senior Project Manager, Cardiovascular Diseases [email protected] Purpose With this call, the Novo Nordisk Foundation wishes to support the cardiometabolic research environment in the Nordic countries, while enhancing the use of ‘Danish Center for Strategic Research in Type 2 Diabetes’ (DD2) data, with a particular focus on promoting DD2 to new and external researchers. The purpose of the call ‘DD2 Research Grant’ is to promote cohort-related research within the field of cardiometabolic diseases (CMDs), while maintaining and improving a unique Danish type 2 diabetes (T2D) resource. Using DD2 data, projects supported by the ‘DD2 Research Grant’ should lead to better understanding, treatment and prevention of T2D complications, its interactions with other CMDs and improved quality of life for people with T2D. To find out more about the DD2 cohort profile, please refer to: https://dd2.dk/forskning/projektoversigt/projekter/dd2-cohort-profile Important information To use DD2 data for a research proposal, you must fill in and send a DD2 protocol which will be evaluated by the DD2 Scientific Advisory Board ( https://dd2.dk/om-dd2/scientific-advisory-board ). If you need guidance in connection with the preparation of the protocol, you are welcome to contact research manager Kurt Højlund ( [email protected] ) and program manager Jens Steen Nielsen ( [email protected] ). You can download the protocol template here or you can obtain the protocol template from DD2. DD2 Symposium To promote the call, DD2 hosts a symposium on 25 October 2024 . The symposium will take place at the Steno Diabetes Center Copenhagen in Herlev. Please contact DD2 for more information and to sign up for the symposium: https://dd2.dk/forskning/seminar Eligibility Projects must be anchored at a hospital, university, or other non-profit research institution in a Nordic country (Denmark, Faroe Islands, Finland, Greenland, Iceland, Norway or Sweden). The profile of main applicants may be experienced basic or experimental researchers or clinicians who are in the process of establishing themselves as independent researchers. Early-career researchers such as senior PhD’s, postdocs and assistant professors are encouraged to apply. Applicants that are new users of DD2 data will be prioritised during the evaluation. Master students are not eligible to apply (as main applicant or co-applicant). As the aim of the call ‘DD2 Research Grant’ is to enhance the use of the DD2 resource to external researchers, applicants and co-applicants cannot be: i) employed by the DD2 organisation or in any way be financially supported by DD2, ii) a member of the DD2 Board of Directors, or iii) a member of the DD2 Scientific Advisory Board. As a main applicant, you may submit only one application for a ‘DD2 Research Grant’ in the same calendar year. If you submit more than one application as a main applicant, only the first application submitted will be evaluated, while any subsequent applications will receive an administrative rejection. Funding A total of up to DKK 24.5 million is available for grants between DKK 1 million and up to DKK 5 million for projects lasting from 1 to 3 years. Co-applicants The research project can comprise a main applicant and up to two co-applicants. You can be a co-applicant on up to two applications for a ‘DD2 Research Grant’ in the same calendar year. Application process As one of the key aims of the ‘DD2 Research Grant’ is to promote the DD2 resource to external researchers, applicants that are new users of DD2 data will be prioritised. Further, early- to mid-career researchers such as postdoctoral fellows, assistant professors, non-tenure-track associate professors will be prioritised. NNF performs screening based on administrative criteria. As part of this screening, the DD2 Scientific Advisory Board will be consulted and review/screen parts of the applications for ethical compliance, duplication of projects, feasibility, etc. Please refer to the guidelines for further instructions.", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:38.233180+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants-and-residencies/aproximacion-al-estar-siendo-approaching-estar-siendo-2/": {"text": "Cancel Main applicant visual artist Pastrana Victoria Amount of funding 9090 € Type of funding Saari Residence Fields Performing arts Visual arts Grant year 2026 If you are this project's responsible person, you can sign in and add more information. Log in Share: Back to Grants listing Application summary My project explores how forms of dwelling, memory, and belonging are reconfigured through movement and displacement. Starting from my ongoing research on migration and Indigenous knowledge, I am interested in how these experiences can be translated into spatial, material, and symbolic structures. During the residency, I will focus on developing a body of research that responds to the surrounding landscape and the experience of inhabiting a temporary place. I am particularly interested in observing how architecture, environment, and everyday gestures shape a sense of belonging, even in conditions of transience. Through writing, drawing, and audiovisual work, I will build a visual and conceptual archive that reflects on fragile forms of shelter, domesticity, and adaptation. Rather than producing final objects, this period will allow me to deepen a process of thinking through materials, gestures, and spatial imaginaries that later unfold into installations.This project is also informed by Indigenous ways of understanding time and existence, particularly the idea of being anchored in the present as an active and ongoing state. In contrast to capitalist temporalities that prioritize productivity and constant projection into the future, I am interested in exploring forms of “being” that emerge through attention, repetition, and embodied experience. In this sense, migration and resilience are not only conditions of displacement, but also ways of generating dialogues with the present, where memory, body, and territory coexist and continuously transform. The project seeks to create a dialogue between personal memory, collective histories, and the specific context of the residency, exploring how ways of living and building meaning shift across territories. Back to Grants listing", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:40.446332+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/apurahat-ja-residenssipaikat/aproximacion-al-estar-siendo-approaching-estar-siendo/": {"text": "Päähakija visual artist Pastrana Victoria Myöntösumma 9090 € Tukimuoto Saaren kartanon residenssi Alat Esittävä taide Kuvataide Myöntövuosi 2026 Jos olet hankkeen vastuuhenkilö, voit kirjautua sisään ja lisätä hankkeen tietoja. Kirjaudu sisään Jaa: Takaisin apurahalistaukseen Hakemuksen tiivistelmä My project explores how forms of dwelling, memory, and belonging are reconfigured through movement and displacement. Starting from my ongoing research on migration and Indigenous knowledge, I am interested in how these experiences can be translated into spatial, material, and symbolic structures. During the residency, I will focus on developing a body of research that responds to the surrounding landscape and the experience of inhabiting a temporary place. I am particularly interested in observing how architecture, environment, and everyday gestures shape a sense of belonging, even in conditions of transience. Through writing, drawing, and audiovisual work, I will build a visual and conceptual archive that reflects on fragile forms of shelter, domesticity, and adaptation. Rather than producing final objects, this period will allow me to deepen a process of thinking through materials, gestures, and spatial imaginaries that later unfold into installations.This project is also informed by Indigenous ways of understanding time and existence, particularly the idea of being anchored in the present as an active and ongoing state. In contrast to capitalist temporalities that prioritize productivity and constant projection into the future, I am interested in exploring forms of “being” that emerge through attention, repetition, and embodied experience. In this sense, migration and resilience are not only conditions of displacement, but also ways of generating dialogues with the present, where memory, body, and territory coexist and continuously transform. The project seeks to create a dialogue between personal memory, collective histories, and the specific context of the residency, exploring how ways of living and building meaning shift across territories. Takaisin apurahalistaukseen", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:42.330057+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants-and-residencies/uuden-paikkasidonnaisen-monitaiteellisen-esityksen-suunnittelu-ja-alkuun-synnyttaminen-tyonimella-kuljettaja-2/": {"text": "Cancel Main applicant Kiltit ihmiset ry and working group Amount of funding 6400 € Type of funding Saari Residence Fields Media and sound art Performing arts Grant year 2026 If you are this project's responsible person, you can sign in and add more information. Log in Share: Back to Grants listing Application summary Olemme ryhmänä tehneet kaksi muualle kuin näyttämölle sijoittuvaa esitystä Rosemaryn huone (2018 Lammassaari) sekä Huone jossa me tavataan (2025 Taidekoti Kirpilä), jotka molemmat pohjautuivat tekstiin, omalakiseen maailmaan ja todella voimakkaaseen kokemukseen katsojille. Lähdemme nyt suunnittelemaan seuraavaa, jossa haluamme panostaa alkuideointiin yhdessä ydinryhmän kanssa sekä antaa itsellemme vapauden leikkiä painottomuuden sekä ei-tietämisen maastossa. Lähtökohtanamme on kuoleman, ja tuonpuoleisen teemat. Rituaalit. Siirtymät. Toden ja epätoden rajapinnat. Uskomukset. Yliluonnollinen. Back to Grants listing", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:44.280780+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/apurahat-ja-residenssipaikat/uuden-paikkasidonnaisen-monitaiteellisen-esityksen-suunnittelu-ja-alkuun-synnyttaminen-tyonimella-kuljettaja/": {"text": "Päähakija Kiltit ihmiset ry ja työryhmä Myöntösumma 6400 € Tukimuoto Saaren kartanon residenssi Alat Esittävä taide Media- ja äänitaide Myöntövuosi 2026 Jos olet hankkeen vastuuhenkilö, voit kirjautua sisään ja lisätä hankkeen tietoja. Kirjaudu sisään Jaa: Takaisin apurahalistaukseen Hakemuksen tiivistelmä Olemme ryhmänä tehneet kaksi muualle kuin näyttämölle sijoittuvaa esitystä Rosemaryn huone (2018 Lammassaari) sekä Huone jossa me tavataan (2025 Taidekoti Kirpilä), jotka molemmat pohjautuivat tekstiin, omalakiseen maailmaan ja todella voimakkaaseen kokemukseen katsojille. Lähdemme nyt suunnittelemaan seuraavaa, jossa haluamme panostaa alkuideointiin yhdessä ydinryhmän kanssa sekä antaa itsellemme vapauden leikkiä painottomuuden sekä ei-tietämisen maastossa. Lähtökohtanamme on kuoleman, ja tuonpuoleisen teemat. Rituaalit. Siirtymät. Toden ja epätoden rajapinnat. Uskomukset. Yliluonnollinen. Takaisin apurahalistaukseen", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:46.210247+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants-and-residencies/a-voice-inside-the-music-2/": {"text": "Cancel Main applicant Composer Wedlund Frej Amount of funding 5400 € Type of funding Saari Residence Fields Music Grant year 2026 If you are this project's responsible person, you can sign in and add more information. Log in Share: Back to Grants listing Application summary My work as a composer centres on fragility, intimacy, and the creation of shared listening situations. This has primarily taken place within instrumental and acoustic music, often through quiet, unstable sound worlds shaped by extended techniques and preparations. In recent years, this practice has expanded to include text, most fully in the concert-length intermedial work Panic diaries (2025). During my residency at Saari, I will focus on a question that emerged directly from that work: what does it mean, in my practice, to compose with voice? Not as narration or phonetic texture, but as material that is present, hesitant, and close to speech. I am interested in the unstable space between speaking and singing, and how that condition interacts with the fragile, acoustically distorted sound worlds I already work with. The work will take the form of small studies involving voice, text, and prepared instruments, approached through recording, writing, and experimentation rather than toward a fixed outcome. Back to Grants listing", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:48.135918+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/apurahat-ja-residenssipaikat/a-voice-inside-the-music/": {"text": "Päähakija Composer Wedlund Frej Myöntösumma 5400 € Tukimuoto Saaren kartanon residenssi Alat Musiikki Myöntövuosi 2026 Jos olet hankkeen vastuuhenkilö, voit kirjautua sisään ja lisätä hankkeen tietoja. Kirjaudu sisään Jaa: Takaisin apurahalistaukseen Hakemuksen tiivistelmä My work as a composer centres on fragility, intimacy, and the creation of shared listening situations. This has primarily taken place within instrumental and acoustic music, often through quiet, unstable sound worlds shaped by extended techniques and preparations. In recent years, this practice has expanded to include text, most fully in the concert-length intermedial work Panic diaries (2025). During my residency at Saari, I will focus on a question that emerged directly from that work: what does it mean, in my practice, to compose with voice? Not as narration or phonetic texture, but as material that is present, hesitant, and close to speech. I am interested in the unstable space between speaking and singing, and how that condition interacts with the fragile, acoustically distorted sound worlds I already work with. The work will take the form of small studies involving voice, text, and prepared instruments, approached through recording, writing, and experimentation rather than toward a fixed outcome. Takaisin apurahalistaukseen", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:50.055368+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants-and-residencies/on-weathering-and-foraging-materials-2/": {"text": "Cancel Main applicant Visual artist Casková Michaela Amount of funding 5400 € Type of funding Saari Residence Fields Visual arts Grant year 2026 If you are this project's responsible person, you can sign in and add more information. Log in Share: Back to Grants listing Application summary My artistic practice explores art as a process, rooted in the forest as collaborator, where walking, observing, and foraging shape the work. Using weather phenomena as both material and metaphor, I combine seasonal foraging, material experimentation, and sensory observation to create infusions, textiles, papers, pigments, and educational tools. Slow, process-driven methods—such as transforming foraged plants—allow reflection on ecological rhythms, and care about our shared futures. During the residency, I will plan and design parts for larger project I am working at – a Mobile Material Library – that functions as both an archive and artwork, reflecting the seasonal, tactile, and ephemeral qualities of natural materials. Throughout the residency, I will continue my foraging and material research, strengthen my commitment to social practice, maintain and expand existing collaborations, and work on upcoming publications: a weather observation archive, forest walk guide, and Forest Cards. Central to my practice is the forests in the past decade, where engagement with local ecosystems informs both material research and activist collaborations across borders. Developed through the long-term project Small Talks (2016–ongoing), my work emphasizes walking, weathering, and responding to natural materials, tracing social and environmental rhythms and connecting audiences to the temporality, responsiveness, and intimacy of forest ecosystems. By combining artistic experimentation, ecological awareness, and participatory methods, I create spaces where foraging, weather observation, and material transformation become tools for reflection, dialogue, and care. Back to Grants listing", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:52.101719+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/apurahat-ja-residenssipaikat/on-weathering-and-foraging-materials/": {"text": "Päähakija Visual artist Casková Michaela Myöntösumma 5400 € Tukimuoto Saaren kartanon residenssi Alat Kuvataide Myöntövuosi 2026 Jos olet hankkeen vastuuhenkilö, voit kirjautua sisään ja lisätä hankkeen tietoja. Kirjaudu sisään Jaa: Takaisin apurahalistaukseen Hakemuksen tiivistelmä My artistic practice explores art as a process, rooted in the forest as collaborator, where walking, observing, and foraging shape the work. Using weather phenomena as both material and metaphor, I combine seasonal foraging, material experimentation, and sensory observation to create infusions, textiles, papers, pigments, and educational tools. Slow, process-driven methods—such as transforming foraged plants—allow reflection on ecological rhythms, and care about our shared futures. During the residency, I will plan and design parts for larger project I am working at – a Mobile Material Library – that functions as both an archive and artwork, reflecting the seasonal, tactile, and ephemeral qualities of natural materials. Throughout the residency, I will continue my foraging and material research, strengthen my commitment to social practice, maintain and expand existing collaborations, and work on upcoming publications: a weather observation archive, forest walk guide, and Forest Cards. Central to my practice is the forests in the past decade, where engagement with local ecosystems informs both material research and activist collaborations across borders. Developed through the long-term project Small Talks (2016–ongoing), my work emphasizes walking, weathering, and responding to natural materials, tracing social and environmental rhythms and connecting audiences to the temporality, responsiveness, and intimacy of forest ecosystems. By combining artistic experimentation, ecological awareness, and participatory methods, I create spaces where foraging, weather observation, and material transformation become tools for reflection, dialogue, and care. Takaisin apurahalistaukseen", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:53.988525+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants-and-residencies/sedimenting-relations-power-folklore-and-non-human-narratives-2/": {"text": "Cancel Main applicant Artist Yadav Ankur Amount of funding 6600 € Type of funding Saari Residence Fields Film Visual arts Grant year 2026 If you are this project's responsible person, you can sign in and add more information. Log in Share: Back to Grants listing Application summary My research at the Saari Residence will explore how humans build relationships, with each other, with systems of power, and with the material world. I am interested in how power operates: how it shapes, constrains, and at times crushes both individuals and the elements that exist within its structures. At the same time, I want to investigate how alternative forms of relation can emerge, forms that are more diverse, porous, and resistant to domination. The project will primarily take the form of writing, accompanied by visual and material explorations. I will focus on narratives drawn from cultural folklore, oral traditions, and ecological observation that speak about transformation, resistance, and coexistence. These stories often hold knowledge about living with uncertainty, negotiating power, and imagining different ways of being together. I am particularly interested in how natural phenomena can be understood as poetic events. Processes such as erosion, sedimentation, and decay will inform both the conceptual and formal aspects of the work. These processes challenge linear and human-centered notions of time and agency, offering instead a perspective where change is continuous, relational, and shared across forms of life and matter. Through this research, I aim to develop a body of writing that reflects on how we can learn from these narratives and phenomena to rethink our own social and ecological structures. The project seeks to open up questions rather than provide fixed answers, inviting reflection on how we might build more inclusive and interconnected ways of living. Back to Grants listing", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:55.867404+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/apurahat-ja-residenssipaikat/sedimenting-relations-power-folklore-and-non-human-narratives/": {"text": "Päähakija Artist Yadav Ankur Myöntösumma 6600 € Tukimuoto Saaren kartanon residenssi Alat Elokuva Kuvataide Myöntövuosi 2026 Jos olet hankkeen vastuuhenkilö, voit kirjautua sisään ja lisätä hankkeen tietoja. Kirjaudu sisään Jaa: Takaisin apurahalistaukseen Hakemuksen tiivistelmä My research at the Saari Residence will explore how humans build relationships, with each other, with systems of power, and with the material world. I am interested in how power operates: how it shapes, constrains, and at times crushes both individuals and the elements that exist within its structures. At the same time, I want to investigate how alternative forms of relation can emerge, forms that are more diverse, porous, and resistant to domination. The project will primarily take the form of writing, accompanied by visual and material explorations. I will focus on narratives drawn from cultural folklore, oral traditions, and ecological observation that speak about transformation, resistance, and coexistence. These stories often hold knowledge about living with uncertainty, negotiating power, and imagining different ways of being together. I am particularly interested in how natural phenomena can be understood as poetic events. Processes such as erosion, sedimentation, and decay will inform both the conceptual and formal aspects of the work. These processes challenge linear and human-centered notions of time and agency, offering instead a perspective where change is continuous, relational, and shared across forms of life and matter. Through this research, I aim to develop a body of writing that reflects on how we can learn from these narratives and phenomena to rethink our own social and ecological structures. The project seeks to open up questions rather than provide fixed answers, inviting reflection on how we might build more inclusive and interconnected ways of living. Takaisin apurahalistaukseen", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:57.906232+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants-and-residencies/the-aesthetics-of-the-occult-2/": {"text": "Cancel Main applicant Master of Fine Arts, artist and curator Bengtsson Maria and working group (The Aesthetics of the Occult // Det Occultas Estetik) Members of the project Other Members of the team: Sanz Rosal Laura, Radomska Marietta, El Arbi Nabila, Nenzelius Per Amount of funding 30490 € Type of funding Saari Residence Fields Visual arts Grant year 2026 If you are this project's responsible person, you can sign in and add more information. Log in Share: Back to Grants listing Application summary The Aesthetics of the Occult is a transdisciplinary network of artists, curators, and researchers working across installation, performance, ecological practice, and speculative narration. The project explores how artistic practices can engage with forces that operate beneath or alongside dominant systems of knowledge through material processes, ritual, and site-responsive work. At Saari Residence, the group will develop a collective working process that moves between individual and shared practices. Drawing on ecofeminist thinking, hauntology, and technomagical perspectives, the project approaches the occult as a complex and layered site of transformation, comparable to processes of composting or fermentation. Through regular collective sessions spanning ritual practices, alternative myth-making and discussion, the artists will explore alternative narratives rooted in embodied and situated knowledge. Site-responsive walks, listening practices, and engagement with local materials will allow the surrounding environment and its more-than-human agencies to act as an active collaborator in the process. The residency focuses on developing a shared methodology rooted in material experimentation, embodied knowledge, and collective thinking. The project creates a framework for ongoing collaboration, where ideas and forms can evolve across contexts and future presentations. Other members of the working group are nabbteeri (Janne Nabb and Maria Teeri), Yvonne Billimore, Sabīne Šnē, Heksebrygg (Hanna Halsebakke and Ajarose Solvild), Martina O'Brien, and Tanja Thorjussen. Back to Grants listing", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:12:59.851118+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/apurahat-ja-residenssipaikat/the-aesthetics-of-the-occult/": {"text": "Päähakija Master of Fine Arts, artist and curator Bengtsson Maria ja työryhmä (The Aesthetics of the Occult // Det Occultas Estetik) Hankkeen jäsenet Ryhmän muut jäsenet: Sanz Rosal Laura, Radomska Marietta, El Arbi Nabila, Nenzelius Per Myöntösumma 30490 € Tukimuoto Saaren kartanon residenssi Alat Kuvataide Myöntövuosi 2026 Jos olet hankkeen vastuuhenkilö, voit kirjautua sisään ja lisätä hankkeen tietoja. Kirjaudu sisään Jaa: Takaisin apurahalistaukseen Hakemuksen tiivistelmä The Aesthetics of the Occult is a transdisciplinary network of artists, curators, and researchers working across installation, performance, ecological practice, and speculative narration. The project explores how artistic practices can engage with forces that operate beneath or alongside dominant systems of knowledge through material processes, ritual, and site-responsive work. At Saari Residence, the group will develop a collective working process that moves between individual and shared practices. Drawing on ecofeminist thinking, hauntology, and technomagical perspectives, the project approaches the occult as a complex and layered site of transformation, comparable to processes of composting or fermentation. Through regular collective sessions spanning ritual practices, alternative myth-making and discussion, the artists will explore alternative narratives rooted in embodied and situated knowledge. Site-responsive walks, listening practices, and engagement with local materials will allow the surrounding environment and its more-than-human agencies to act as an active collaborator in the process. The residency focuses on developing a shared methodology rooted in material experimentation, embodied knowledge, and collective thinking. The project creates a framework for ongoing collaboration, where ideas and forms can evolve across contexts and future presentations. Other members of the working group are nabbteeri (Janne Nabb and Maria Teeri), Yvonne Billimore, Sabīne Šnē, Heksebrygg (Hanna Halsebakke and Ajarose Solvild), Martina O'Brien, and Tanja Thorjussen. Takaisin apurahalistaukseen", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:13:01.753827+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants-and-residencies/necessary-clarity-of-the-overflow-a-cartography-of-the-present-2/": {"text": "Cancel Main applicant Writer Shan Xiao Yue Amount of funding 5400 € Type of funding Saari Residence Fields Literature Grant year 2026 If you are this project's responsible person, you can sign in and add more information. Log in Share: Back to Grants listing Application summary Necessary Clarity of the Overflow: A Cartography of the Present is a project that merges the ancient cartographic drive with contemporary ideas of placeness, to create a series of lyrical essays that note the vertical richness of each discrete space, layered as they are with the tulle of histories, stories, and memories. Maps are the same as every human creation—burdened with our predispositions, customs, and desires. From indigenous Australian songlines and ancient Chinese mythology, the navigational tools and techniques that human beings have developed form an immense and variegated body of knowledge, one that draws a portrait not only of how the landscape has changed, but how the human body has long existed as a spatial orientation. I’m interested in how our current notions of place, led almost universally by technological guidance and pre-ordained pathways, disrupt humanity’s natural creative instinct for exploration, curiosity, and engagement with their environment—how literature can be utilised to confront this drive towards flatness and the extinction of intimate detail. As concerns of earthly degradation and deterioration increase in the Anthropocene, our detachment from environment creates a mindset that focuses largely on defense—shielding ourselves in shelters and manmade oases against the climate, sketched with morbid terms in perceivable threats. I would like to create works that instead imagine a more intimate and delicate movement through our world—one that values knowledge instead of alienation. I am working from the essential idea that our carelessness can be alleviated by an awareness of places as immense archives of beauty, conquering, surrender, and speech. Back to Grants listing", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:13:03.702447+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/apurahat-ja-residenssipaikat/necessary-clarity-of-the-overflow-a-cartography-of-the-present/": {"text": "Päähakija Writer Shan Xiao Yue Myöntösumma 5400 € Tukimuoto Saaren kartanon residenssi Alat Kirjallisuus Myöntövuosi 2026 Jos olet hankkeen vastuuhenkilö, voit kirjautua sisään ja lisätä hankkeen tietoja. Kirjaudu sisään Jaa: Takaisin apurahalistaukseen Hakemuksen tiivistelmä Necessary Clarity of the Overflow: A Cartography of the Present is a project that merges the ancient cartographic drive with contemporary ideas of placeness, to create a series of lyrical essays that note the vertical richness of each discrete space, layered as they are with the tulle of histories, stories, and memories. Maps are the same as every human creation—burdened with our predispositions, customs, and desires. From indigenous Australian songlines and ancient Chinese mythology, the navigational tools and techniques that human beings have developed form an immense and variegated body of knowledge, one that draws a portrait not only of how the landscape has changed, but how the human body has long existed as a spatial orientation. I’m interested in how our current notions of place, led almost universally by technological guidance and pre-ordained pathways, disrupt humanity’s natural creative instinct for exploration, curiosity, and engagement with their environment—how literature can be utilised to confront this drive towards flatness and the extinction of intimate detail. As concerns of earthly degradation and deterioration increase in the Anthropocene, our detachment from environment creates a mindset that focuses largely on defense—shielding ourselves in shelters and manmade oases against the climate, sketched with morbid terms in perceivable threats. I would like to create works that instead imagine a more intimate and delicate movement through our world—one that values knowledge instead of alienation. I am working from the essential idea that our carelessness can be alleviated by an awareness of places as immense archives of beauty, conquering, surrender, and speech. Takaisin apurahalistaukseen", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:13:05.667703+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants-and-residencies/available-futures-boreal-cartographies-for-getting-lost-2/": {"text": "Cancel Main applicant Visual Artist Guzman Diaz Sebastian Amount of funding 8420 € Type of funding Saari Residence Fields Visual arts Grant year 2026 If you are this project's responsible person, you can sign in and add more information. Log in Share: Back to Grants listing Application summary At the Saari Residence, I will work on Available Futures: Boreal Cartographies for Getting Lost, an investigation into the landscape of Mynämäki: its organisms, its material fragments, its layers of time; through drift as method and sensory cartography as result. For six years I worked as an engineer on the construction of hydroelectric plants in Colombia. From inside that system I learned to read with technical precision how a territory transforms: what disappears, what resists, what persists beneath what progress considers finished. That experience is the engine of my artistic practice. Since then, my work has been a way of walking slowly through transformed landscapes to see what remains. In the Atacama Desert I found microorganisms surviving conditions we would consider uninhabitable. In Buenos Aires I found the remains of reconfigured territories holding memories no one had named. The question that drives me is always the same: what persists? What forms of life, memory, and landscape survive outside the dominant narrative? Available Futures is a concept I developed in previous projects. Futures are not unique, linear, or predetermined; they are latent, multiple, and situated. The future for Finland is not the same as for Colombia. Saari would be the next chapter: the same question, a radically different landscape, and therefore, its own materializations. During the residency I will produce hand-drawn sensory cartographies and a manuscript of speculative texts moving between observation and imagination. The first two weeks are for arrival and exploration, walking without destination, listening. The middle weeks for sustained fieldwork and writing. The final two weeks for editing and letting what was found settle. Throughout, I look forward to the conversations, the weekly presentations, and the unexpected directions that other residents might open. Back to Grants listing", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:13:07.583633+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/apurahat-ja-residenssipaikat/available-futures-boreal-cartographies-for-getting-lost/": {"text": "Päähakija Visual Artist Guzman Diaz Sebastian Myöntösumma 8420 € Tukimuoto Saaren kartanon residenssi Alat Kuvataide Myöntövuosi 2026 Jos olet hankkeen vastuuhenkilö, voit kirjautua sisään ja lisätä hankkeen tietoja. Kirjaudu sisään Jaa: Takaisin apurahalistaukseen Hakemuksen tiivistelmä At the Saari Residence, I will work on Available Futures: Boreal Cartographies for Getting Lost, an investigation into the landscape of Mynämäki: its organisms, its material fragments, its layers of time; through drift as method and sensory cartography as result. For six years I worked as an engineer on the construction of hydroelectric plants in Colombia. From inside that system I learned to read with technical precision how a territory transforms: what disappears, what resists, what persists beneath what progress considers finished. That experience is the engine of my artistic practice. Since then, my work has been a way of walking slowly through transformed landscapes to see what remains. In the Atacama Desert I found microorganisms surviving conditions we would consider uninhabitable. In Buenos Aires I found the remains of reconfigured territories holding memories no one had named. The question that drives me is always the same: what persists? What forms of life, memory, and landscape survive outside the dominant narrative? Available Futures is a concept I developed in previous projects. Futures are not unique, linear, or predetermined; they are latent, multiple, and situated. The future for Finland is not the same as for Colombia. Saari would be the next chapter: the same question, a radically different landscape, and therefore, its own materializations. During the residency I will produce hand-drawn sensory cartographies and a manuscript of speculative texts moving between observation and imagination. The first two weeks are for arrival and exploration, walking without destination, listening. The middle weeks for sustained fieldwork and writing. The final two weeks for editing and letting what was found settle. Throughout, I look forward to the conversations, the weekly presentations, and the unexpected directions that other residents might open. Takaisin apurahalistaukseen", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:13:09.444305+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants-and-residencies/as-i-change-my-memory-changes-assemblages-translating-memory-and-lived-experience-2/": {"text": "Cancel Main applicant Doctor in Arts Coyotzi Borja Andrea Amount of funding 7600 € Type of funding Saari Residence Fields Media and sound art Visual arts Grant year 2026 If you are this project's responsible person, you can sign in and add more information. Log in Share: Back to Grants listing Application summary The project “As I Change My Memory Changes: assemblages translating memory and lived experience” is about how memory, identity, and experience shift as I change and grow. I explore my relationship with everyday objects such as a burnt piece of rug, a strawberry bedsheet, socks, porcelain figures, and drawings. Each object carries a memory, a fragment of who I was and how I lived. These memories are translated constantly. I collect and assemble objects, then transform them into writing and audio. Translation here is not just about language but about converting, explaining, interpreting, and transferring experience from one form, state, or place to another. I pay attention to the infraordinary, the everyday life that often goes unnoticed, following the ideas of George Perec. In my work, small actions reveal memory and desire. For example, I framed socks I wanted to see because I could not use them otherwise. In Helsinki, I noticed the Alepa cups with cheerful faces in the streets, worn and dirty, which made me curious about their visibility and condition. These moments show how memory and objects interact and shape the way the world is inhabited. Back to Grants listing", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:13:11.423207+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/apurahat-ja-residenssipaikat/as-i-change-my-memory-changes-assemblages-translating-memory-and-lived-experience/": {"text": "Päähakija Doctor in Arts Coyotzi Borja Andrea Myöntösumma 7600 € Tukimuoto Saaren kartanon residenssi Alat Kuvataide Media- ja äänitaide Myöntövuosi 2026 Jos olet hankkeen vastuuhenkilö, voit kirjautua sisään ja lisätä hankkeen tietoja. Kirjaudu sisään Jaa: Takaisin apurahalistaukseen Hakemuksen tiivistelmä The project “As I Change My Memory Changes: assemblages translating memory and lived experience” is about how memory, identity, and experience shift as I change and grow. I explore my relationship with everyday objects such as a burnt piece of rug, a strawberry bedsheet, socks, porcelain figures, and drawings. Each object carries a memory, a fragment of who I was and how I lived. These memories are translated constantly. I collect and assemble objects, then transform them into writing and audio. Translation here is not just about language but about converting, explaining, interpreting, and transferring experience from one form, state, or place to another. I pay attention to the infraordinary, the everyday life that often goes unnoticed, following the ideas of George Perec. In my work, small actions reveal memory and desire. For example, I framed socks I wanted to see because I could not use them otherwise. In Helsinki, I noticed the Alepa cups with cheerful faces in the streets, worn and dirty, which made me curious about their visibility and condition. These moments show how memory and objects interact and shape the way the world is inhabited. Takaisin apurahalistaukseen", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:13:13.313399+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/en/grants-and-residencies/zeybek-2/": {"text": "Cancel Main applicant Artist Esra Cinar and artist Anil Temur Amount of funding 12300 € Type of funding Saari Residence Fields Film Grant year 2026 If you are this project's responsible person, you can sign in and add more information. Log in Share: Back to Grants listing Application summary Zeybek is a feature film about two queer women, one in Turkey, the other in England, who meet online and fall into a long-distance relationship. As their connection deepens, so does the desire to cross the border between them. But crossing is not the same from both sides. They live in entirely different worlds. What is a dream for one is taken for granted by the other. Through each other, both women begin to see what had remained out of view. The woman in Turkey starts to recognise how different a life can be, how it might expand. The woman in England, for the first time, confronts how much she has never had to fight for, while also discovering a sense of closeness, care, and belonging that feels increasingly distant in her own world. The film follows their attempt to close the distance between them, and everything that stands in the way: cultural divides, bureaucracy, suppression, systems that were never built with love in mind. Zeybek asks whether love is enough to cross that distance, and what it costs to try. The formal concept mirrors the story. Two directors each create one side of the film, working from within their character’s world. One films Turkey. One films England. Neither directs the other’s work. Each world is shaped through a distinct gaze, allowing the distance between them to be felt not only in the narrative, but in every frame. In post-production, these two visual languages, rhythms, and realities are brought together into a single film. As far as we know, this approach has no direct precedent in cinema. Separation becoming connection. Over two months at Saari, we will develop the film's narrative structure, write a full treatment, and complete a first draft of the screenplay, alongside a project framework to support its next stage of funding and production. (Zeybek is a Turkish word, an Aegean warrior dance named after outlaws who stood against injustice, with slow and sweeping movements resembling the flight of a hawk) Back to Grants listing", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:13:15.215570+00:00"}, "https://koneensaatio.fi/apurahat-ja-residenssipaikat/zeybek/": {"text": "Päähakija Artist Cinar Esra and artist Temur Anil Myöntösumma 12300 € Tukimuoto Saaren kartanon residenssi Alat Elokuva Myöntövuosi 2026 Jos olet hankkeen vastuuhenkilö, voit kirjautua sisään ja lisätä hankkeen tietoja. Kirjaudu sisään Jaa: Takaisin apurahalistaukseen Hakemuksen tiivistelmä Zeybek is a feature film about two queer women, one in Turkey, the other in England, who meet online and fall into a long-distance relationship. As their connection deepens, so does the desire to cross the border between them. But crossing is not the same from both sides. They live in entirely different worlds. What is a dream for one is taken for granted by the other. Through each other, both women begin to see what had remained out of view. The woman in Turkey starts to recognise how different a life can be, how it might expand. The woman in England, for the first time, confronts how much she has never had to fight for, while also discovering a sense of closeness, care, and belonging that feels increasingly distant in her own world. The film follows their attempt to close the distance between them, and everything that stands in the way: cultural divides, bureaucracy, suppression, systems that were never built with love in mind. Zeybek asks whether love is enough to cross that distance, and what it costs to try. The formal concept mirrors the story. Two directors each create one side of the film, working from within their character’s world. One films Turkey. One films England. Neither directs the other’s work. Each world is shaped through a distinct gaze, allowing the distance between them to be felt not only in the narrative, but in every frame. In post-production, these two visual languages, rhythms, and realities are brought together into a single film. As far as we know, this approach has no direct precedent in cinema. Separation becoming connection. Over two months at Saari, we will develop the film's narrative structure, write a full treatment, and complete a first draft of the screenplay, alongside a project framework to support its next stage of funding and production. (Zeybek is a Turkish word, an Aegean warrior dance named after outlaws who stood against injustice, with slow and sweeping movements resembling the flight of a hawk) Takaisin apurahalistaukseen", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:13:17.148047+00:00"}, "https://www.daimler-benz-stiftung.de/format/bertha-benz-preis/": {"text": "Die Daimler und Benz Stiftung zeichnet mit dem „Bertha-Benz-Preis“ jährlich eine Ingenieurin aus, die eine herausragende Promotion in Deutschland mit Dr.-Ing. abgeschlossen hat. Ziel ist es, auf die Leistungen von Frauen in den Ingenieurwissenschaften hinzuweisen und diese zu ermuntern, nach einem Studium dieser Fachdisziplinen eine wissenschaftliche Karriere einzuschlagen. Der Preis ist mit 15.000 Euro dotiert. Kontakt E-Mail Geschützte E-Mail Telefon +49 6203 1092-0 Zeitlicher Ablauf November 2026 bis März 2027 Nominierungsphase In diesem Zeitraum können Nominierungen eingereicht werden. März - April 2027 Auswahlverfahren In diesem Zeitraum wird die Preisträgerin ausgewählt. Juni 2027 Preisverleihung Die Verleihung des Bertha-Benz-Preises findet voraussichtlich im Juni 2027 in Heidelberg statt.", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:58:46.082267+00:00"}, "https://koerber-stiftung.de/projekte/koerber-preis-fuer-die-europaeische-wissenschaft/alle-preistraeger-innen/2026-sara-wickstroem/": {"text": "Foto: Marcus Gloger/Körber-Stiftung i Sara Wickström Sara Wickström erhält Körber-Preis 2026 Die finnische Medizinerin und Zellbiologin Sara Wickström erhält den mit einer Million Euro dotierten Körber-Preis für die Europäische Wissenschaft. Sie entdeckte einen bislang unbekannten Mechanismus, mit dem Zellen ihre Umgebung wahrnehmen: Zellen „fühlen“ physische Kräfte wie Druck oder Zug und können sie bis in ihr Erbgut hinein verarbeiten. So können allein durch physische Einwirkung einzelne Gene an- oder ausgeschaltet werden – ein entscheidender Faktor dafür, wie sich eine Zelle entwickelt oder wie Gewebe nach Verletzungen heilt. Damit gilt Wickström als eine der Begründerinnen der Zellkern-Mechanobiologie, die den Einfluss physischer Kräfte auf Zellen untersucht. Langfristig kann ihre Forschung zur Entwicklung neuer Therapien gegen Krebs, Organvernarbungen und andere, häufig altersbedingte Erkrankungen beitragen. Die verborgenen Sinne der Zellen Die Preisträgerin Durchbrüche kennen keine Disziplingrenzen Urteilsbegründung für den Körber-Preis 2026 Die verborgenen Sinne der Zellen Text: Linda Geddes Sara Wickström ist eine Pionierin auf dem Gebiet der Mechanobiologie des Zellkerns. Die finnische Medizinerin und Zellbiologin hat aufgedeckt, wie physikalische Kräfte wie Dehnung, Kompression und Spannung von Zellen wahrgenommen und in molekulare Signale umgewandelt werden, die die Genaktivität steuern. Ihre Forschung zeigt, wie diese Kräfte die im Chromatin des Zellkerns verpackte Erbinformation umformen und bestimmen, wie sich Zellen entwickeln, regenerieren oder auf Krankheiten reagieren. Diese Erkenntnisse eröffnen neue Wege für die Behandlung von Krebs, Organvernarbungen und anderen altersbedingten Erkrankungen. Mit den Mitteln des Körber-Preises will Wickström untersuchen, ob Zellen ein „Gedächtnis“ für Verletzungen und mechanische Belastungen entwickeln und ob sich dies im Rahmen zukünftiger Therapien beeinflussen lässt. Als Sara Wickström zum ersten Mal durch ein Mikroskop auf das innere Skelett einer Zelle blickte, war sie von dessen Schönheit beeindruckt. Lange Filamente durchzogen das Zellinnere und endeten an den Außenrändern in leuchtenden, tropfenförmigen Haftstellen, an denen sich die Zelle an ihrer Umgebung festhielt. Mit fluoreszierenden Farbstoffen und Antikörpern chemisch angefärbt, leuchteten sie neonfarben in der Dunkelheit und enthüllten eine verborgene Welt, deren Bedeutung sich gerade erst abzuzeichnen begann. Damals verglichen Lehrbücher die Fasern und Verbindungen dieses „Zytoskeletts“ oft mit den Trägern und Kabeln eines Gebäudes: ein inneres Gerüst, das den Zellen half, ihre Form zu bewahren, sich durch Gewebe zu bewegen und den Transport von Molekülen innerhalb der Zelle zu organisieren. Doch diese Sichtweise begann sich zu wandeln. Wissenschaftlerinnen und Wissenschaftler begannen zu erkennen, dass das Zytoskelett weit mehr tat, als nur Struktur zu bieten. Verbunden mit der Außenwelt durch Ankerpunkte, sogenannte fokale Adhäsionen, und angetrieben von winzigen molekularen Motoren, die mit ATP betrieben werden, ermöglichte das Zytoskelett den Zellen auch, ihre Umgebung physisch wahrzunehmen – Steifigkeit, Spannung und Kompression zu erfassen und entsprechend zu reagieren. In den folgenden zwei Jahrzehnten trug Wickström dazu bei, aufzudecken, wie tiefgreifend diese verborgenen physikalischen Kräfte tatsächlich sind. Ihre Arbeit zeigte, dass äußere mechanische Belastungen, die durch die Umgebung einer Zelle ausgelöst werden, bis in den Zellkern vordringen können, wo sie die Anordnung der DNA neu organisieren und die Genexpression selbst verändern. Es stellte sich heraus, dass Zellen nicht nur auf genetische Anweisungen und chemische Signale reagieren. Sie interpretieren auch kontinuierlich die physikalische Umgebung, in der sie sich befinden: Sie nehmen diese wahr, passen sich an und behalten sie manchmal sogar im Gedächtnis. „Sara Wickström hat durch ihre bahnbrechende Arbeit das Verständnis der Integration mechanischer und biochemischer Informationen in lebendem Gewebe neu definiert und dadurch die moderne Zellbiologie geprägt“, sagt Edvard Moser, Vorsitzender des Körber-Preis Search Committee Life Sciences. Ihre Entdeckungen verändern nun die Sichtweise der Wissenschaft auf Stammzellen, Regeneration, Krebs und Alterung und eröffnen gleichzeitig neue Möglichkeiten für die Diagnose und Behandlung von Krankheiten, die mit einer abnormalen Gewebemechanik zusammenhängen, darunter Fibrose – ein pathologisches Überwachsen von Narbengewebe, das die Organfunktion zunehmend beeinträchtigt und zu vielen altersbedingten Erkrankungen beiträgt. Mit den Mitteln des Körber-Preises will Wickström untersuchen, warum diese abnormalen Gewebezustände fortbestehen und ob es möglich ist, sie umzuprogrammieren, um eine gesunde Gewebefunktion anstelle einer chronischen Vernarbung zu fördern. „Sara Wickström hat durch ihre bahnbrechende Arbeit das Verständnis der Integration mechanischer und biochemischer In", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:58:47.716913+00:00"}, "https://koerber-stiftung.de/projekte/boy-gobert-preis/boy-gobert-preis-2026-an-oscar-hoppe/": {"text": "Foto: Kerstin Schomburg i Oscar Hoppe, Boy-Gobert-Preis 2026 ... Boy-Gobert-Preis 2026 an Oscar Hoppe Der 1996 geborene Schauspieler ist aktuell am Ernst Deutsch Theater Hamburg in der Produktion Keine Aufstiegsgeschichte in gleich mehreren Rollen zu sehen. „Oscar Hoppe ist ein beeindruckend virtuoser Schauspieler, der ein faszinierendes Gespür dafür hat, uns in die unterschiedlichsten Lebenssituationen seiner Figuren hineinzuziehen. Seine Bühnenpräsenz ist magnetisierend“, begründet die Jury unter dem Vorsitz der Schauspielerin Catrin Striebeck ihre Entscheidung. „In Keine Aufstiegsgeschichte entfaltet Oscar Hoppe auf fesselnde Weise eine Vielfalt an Figuren, denen er mit unbekümmerter Leichtigkeit seine kluge Fantasie zur Verfügung stellt. Angstfrei, mit feinem Humor und großer Musikalität riskiert er viel, um die Menschen auf der Bühne und im Publikum zu inspirieren. Seine Energie ist in jedem Moment der Aufführung spürbar.“ Preisverleihung Eva Nemela, Mitglied im Vorstand der Körber-Stiftung, wird Oscar Hoppe die mit 10.000 Euro dotierte Auszeichnung am 29. November 2026 um 11 Uhr im Rahmen einer feierlichen Matinee mit künstlerischen Programmpunkten des Preisträgers, einem Grußwort von Kultursenator Carsten Brosda und weiteren Redebeiträgen im Thalia Theater überreichen. Tickets: 5 € über den Kartenservice des Thalia Theater Vorverkaufsbeginn ist Anfang Oktober. Die Einnahmen aus der Boy-Gobert-Preisverleihung kommen der Spendeninitiative „Der spendierte Platz“ zugute, die Kindern und Jugendlichen, die sich keine Eintrittskarte leisten können, einen kostenlosen Vorstellungsbesuch im Thalia Theater ermöglicht. Für jedes gekaufte Ticket spendet die Körber-Stiftung weitere 5 € für diese Initiative. Medienpartner der Preisverleihung sind NDR 90,3 und Hamburg Journal. Sie wollen die nächste Boy-Gobert-Preisverleihung nicht verpassen? Anmeldung zum Newsletter Zum Preisträger Oscar Hoppe wurde 1996 in Dresden geboren. Bereits während des Studiums an der Hochschule für Schauspielkunst Ernst Busch in Berlin stand er am Berliner Ensemble auf der Bühne. Nach dem Schauspielstudium begann er seine Theaterlaufbahn am Mecklenburgischen Staatstheater. Hier spielte er u.a. Gundermann in Gundermann – Männer, Frauen und Maschinen (R. Patrick Wengenroth), den Wurm in Schillers Kabale und Liebe (R. Steffi Kühnert) sowie George in Von Mäusen und Menschen (R. Martin Nimz). Außerdem steht er regelmäßig für Film- und Fernsehproduktionen vor der Kamera, u.a. für 9 Perfect Strangers , Drifter und Alles Licht, das wir nicht sehen . Darüber hinaus ist er als Sänger und Produzent des Musikduos lele&os aktiv und komponiert für Film und Theater. In der Spielzeit 2025/2026 war er in Keine Aufstiegsgeschichte (R. Marco Damghani) am Hamburger Ernst Deutsch Theater zu sehen. Die Produktion steht ab 1. September 2026 wieder auf dem Spielplan. Fotos zum Preisträger Die Jury Catrin Striebeck (Schauspielerin und Juryvorsitzende) Peter Helling (Journalist) Julia Nachtmann (Schauspielerin und Boy-Gobert-Preisträgerin 2006) Mitra Nadjmabadi (Bühnenbildnerin) Stephan Schad (Schauspieler) Maike Schiller (Journalistin) András Siebold (Dramaturg und Festivalleiter) Sonja Valentin (Dramaturgin)", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:58:49.502843+00:00"}, "https://koerber-stiftung.de/mediathek/koerber-preis-2026-preistraegerin-sara-wickstroem-im-gespraech/": {"text": "Externes Video von YouTube laden. Mehr dazu in unserer Datenschutzerklärung . Körber-Preis 2026: Preisträgerin Sara Wickström im Gespräch Am 18. September 2026 erhielt Sara Wickström den mit einer Million Euro dotierten Körber-Preis für die Europäische Wissenschaft im Hamburger Rathaus. 22. Sep 2026 33 Min. Die finnische Medizinerin und Zellbiologin ist eine Pionierin auf dem Gebiet der Mechanobiologie des Zellkerns. Sie hat aufgedeckt, wie physikalische Kräfte wie Dehnung, Kompression und Spannung von Zellen wahrgenommen und in molekulare Signale umgewandelt werden, die die Genaktivität steuern. Weil Sie „Körber-Preis 2026: Preisträgerin Sara Wickström im Gespräch“ gesehen haben Tage des Exils Frankfurt: Auftakt mit der Rede zum Exil von Irina Scherbakowa Trotz der Kriminalisierung der mit dem Friedensnobelpreis… Autoritäre Zeiten: Die Sehnsucht nach Ordnung In Krisenzeiten wenden sich Menschen nicht selten… The Future of Public History in an A.I.-Dominated World – EUSTORY Past Forward Festival 2026 Jason Steinhauer delivers a fascinating keynote speech…", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:58:51.327333+00:00"}, "https://koerber-stiftung.de/mediathek/tage-des-exils-frankfurt-auftakt-mit-der-rede-zum-exil-von-irina-scherbakowa/": {"text": "Externes Video von YouTube laden. Mehr dazu in unserer Datenschutzerklärung . Tage des Exils Frankfurt: Auftakt mit der Rede zum Exil von Irina Scherbakowa Trotz der Kriminalisierung der mit dem Friedensnobelpreis ausgezeichneten NGO MEMORIAL durch die russische Justiz setzt sich Gründungsmitglied Irina Scherbakowa auch im deutschen Exil gegen staatlich gelenktes Vergessen ein. In diesem Jahr übernimmt sie die Schirmherrschaft der Tage des Exils. Tage des Exils Demokratie Exil Identität Kunst 21. Sep 2026 106 Min. Vom 20. August bis 6. September finden zum zweiten Mal die Tage des Exils in Frankfurt am Main statt, erneut in Kooperation mit dem Deutschen Exilarchiv 1933-1945 der Deutschen Nationalbibliothek. 50 Veranstaltungen in der ganzen Stadt laden auf vielfältige Weise dazu ein, sich mit dem Thema Exil auseinanderzusetzen. Feierlich eröffnet werden die Tage des Exils in der Deutschen Nationalbibliothek mit der Rede zum Exil der diesjährigen Schirmherrin Irina Scherbakowa . Die Mitbegründerin der Menschenrechtsorganisation MEMORIAL hat sich der Dokumentation staatlicher Repression in Russland verschrieben. Mit der Aufarbeitung der Verbrechen des Gulag-Systems und dem Einsatz für zivilgesellschaftliche Erinnerungskultur stellt sie sich der offiziellen russischen Geschichtsdeutung entgegen. Musikalisch abgerundet wird der Abend von der international bekannten Klezmer-Band Dobranotch . Es moderiert Anna Engel von hr2-kultur. Eine Anmeldung zur Veranstaltung ist erforderlich: www.dnb.de/tagedesexils Eine Kooperationsveranstaltung der Körber-Stiftung mit dem Deutschen Exilarchiv 1933-1945. Weitere Informationen zum Programm finden Sie unter Tage des Exils . Weil Sie „Tage des Exils Frankfurt: Auftakt mit der Rede zum Exil von Irina Scherbakowa“ gesehen haben Ohr zur Welt: Matria Matria verbindet Jodeln, alpenländische Lieder und… Autoritäre Zeiten: Die Sehnsucht nach Ordnung In Krisenzeiten wenden sich Menschen nicht selten… Shrinking Civic Space – EUSTORY Past Forward Festival 2026 Experts from Poland, Hungary, and Germany discuss means…", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:58:53.042250+00:00"}, "https://koerber-stiftung.de/podcasts/listen-to-wissen/": {"text": "Listen to Wissen Illustration: Lea Dohle / Basics09 ... Listen to Wissen geht in die dritte Staffel. Ab dem 1. April erscheint alle zwei Wochen eine neue Folge, in der Alumni des Deutschen Studienpreises von ihrer Forschung berichten. Dieses Mal unter anderem zu Nanorobotern in der Blutbahn, Drogenkriegen in den USA und Gesundheit in Krisenzeiten. Mit dem Deutschen Studienpreis zeichnen wir jedes Jahr herausragende Promovierte und ihre Arbeiten aus. Im Gespräch mit Host Liz Remter von ByteFM sprechen junge Forschende über ihre Arbeit, über Musik, berichten, was sie antreibt – und wie ihr Wissen die Gesellschaft besser macht. Dieser Podcast ist ihre Bühne. In Zusammenarbeit mit ByteFM erscheint Listen to Wissen auch im Radioprogramm: Um 12 Uhr zweiwöchentlich ab dem 1. April auf ByteFM und auf allen Podcastplattformen. Die neuesten Folgen Internationale Organisationen: Was passiert, wenn sich die USA zurückziehen? Listen to Wissen Drogenpolitik: Warum scheitern Verbote? Listen to Wissen Lebenszeichen: Welche Rolle spielen Medien in Notfällen? Listen to Wissen Alle Folgen Internationale Organisationen: Was passiert, wenn sich die USA zurückziehen? 47 Min. 10. Jun 2026 Drogenpolitik: Warum scheitern Verbote? 54 Min. 27. Mai 2026 Lebenszeichen: Welche Rolle spielen Medien in Notfällen? 47 Min. 13. Mai 2026 Erbgut: Lassen sich Gendefekte gezielt korrigieren? 46 Min. 29. Apr 2026 Wendezeit: Wie beeinflussen Krisen die Gesundheit? 46 Min. 15. Apr 2026 Nanoroboter: Wie unterstützen sie die Medizin? 45 Min. 1. Apr 2026 Integration: Welche psychologische Versorgung brauchen Geflüchtete? 42 Min. 11. Jun 2025 Bindegewebe: Welche Rolle spielt es im Kampf gegen Krebs? 31 Min. 28. Mai 2025 Gefühlsgemeinschaft: Warum zieht es Menschen zur AfD? 38 Min. 14. Mai 2025 Wohltätigkeit: Wie organisiert sich die Zivilgesellschaft in Saudi-Arabien? 46 Min. 30. Apr 2025 HIV: Welche Hoffnung bringt der Antikörper “1-18” in der Therapie? 47 Min. 16. Apr 2025 Chancengleichheit: Warum haben Kinder keine Lobby? 60 Min. 2. Apr 2025 Cyberkonflikte: Wie machen sich Regierungen Hacker zunutze? 47 Min. 12. Jun 2024 Populismus: Welche Rolle spielt er in der politischen Kommunikation? 49 Min. 29. Mai 2024 Tiere: Welche streicheln und welche schlachten wir? 51 Min. 15. Mai 2024 Ziviler Ungehorsam: Was kann er bewirken? 40 Min. 1. Mai 2024 Virenfalle: Wie fängt man Krankheitserreger im Körper? 36 Min. 17. Apr 2024 Femizide: Wie verurteilen Gerichte Partnerinnentötung? 51 Min. 3. Apr 2024 Trailer: Listen to Wissen 1:06 Min. 26. Mrz 2024 Alle 19 Episoden zeigen Bei Spotify hören zur Website Bei Apple Podcast hören zur Website Bei YouTube hören zur Website Deutscher Studienpreis Deutscher Studienpreis Mit dem Deutschen Studienpreis zeichnen wir jährlich die besten Promovierten aller Fachrichtungen aus. Dabei zählt vor allem die gesellschaftliche Bedeutung der Forschung. Deutscher Studienpreis Kontakt Nina Ritter Programm-Managerin Deutscher Studienpreis ritter@koerber-stiftung.de +49 40 80 81 92 150", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:58:54.875798+00:00"}, "https://www.ki.nrw/infoveranstaltung-einsparpotenziale-im-warenbestand-und-aufruf-zur-mitwirkung-an-warenfluss-studie/": {"text": "News-Eintrag · KI.NRW-News Aufruf zur Mitwirkung an Warenfluss-Studie von KI.NRW 30.06.2025 Handelsverband NRW bietet in Kooperation mit KI.NRW und Fraunhofer IAIS kostenfreien Kostencheck an und ruft zur Teilnahme an Studie zu KI-Warenflusssteuerung auf An drei Terminen im Januar und Februar 2025 lud der Handelsverband Nordrhein-Westfalen zusammen mit KI.NRW und dem Fraunhofer-Institut für Intelligente Analyse- und Informationssysteme IAIS interessierte Handelsunternehmen zur kostenfreien Online-Infoveranstaltung zum Thema »Einsparpotenziale im Warenbestand« ein. Dort stellten sie auch ihre Warenfluss-Studie vor, an der interessierte Handelsunternehmen die Möglichkeit haben, mitzuwirken. Auf den Aufruf haben sich bereits zahlreiche Unternehmen gemeldet. Jedoch können interessierte Unternehmen sich auch weiterhin für die kostenfreie Teilnahme an der Studie registrieren . Die Optimierung des Warenbestands birgt für Händlerinnen und Händler ein hohes Einsparpotenzial – auch für kleine und mittelgroße Einzelhandelsunternehmen. Welches Effizienz- und Umsatzpotenzial steckt also in einer Automatisierung durch KI? Wo kann diese Automatisierung Händlerinnen und Händlern Mehrwerte bringen? Und was wird sich durch den KI-Einsatz im Warenfluss ändern? Auf diese Fragen gingen die Expert*innen von Fraunhofer IAIS und KI.NRW während der Online-Veranstaltungen im Januar und Februar ein. Zudem stellten Maren Brandt und Tim Wirtz (beide von Fraunhofer IAIS) und Dr. Christian Temath (KI.NRW) den Kosten- und Potenzialcheck vor – ein bei Fraunhofer IAIS entwickeltes KI-Tool, das kostenfrei zur Verfügung steht. Sie erläuterten, wie das Tool funktioniert und wie die Überprüfung einer möglichen KI-Unterstützung abläuft. Außerdem stellten die Expert*innen ihre gemeinsame Studie zum Thema »KI im Warenfluss« vor, in der auf Basis der Daten von Handelsunternehmen untersucht wird, welches Potenzial es in der Branche zum Thema Warenfluss gibt. Interessierte Händlerinnen und Händler erfuhren im Rahmen der Veranstaltung, wie sie durch die kostenfreie Teilnahme an der Studie den ersten Schritt in Richtung KI in der Warenflusssteuerung gehen können. Die Studienteilnehmenden erhalten einen kostenfreien Zugang zu Exper*innen-Wissen, eine transparente Kostenanalyse und eine Entscheidungsgrundlage für den KI-Einsatz im Unternehmen. Registrierung Warenfluss-Studie ‹ Zurück zur News-Eintrag-Übersicht", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:58:56.674927+00:00"}, "https://www.ki.nrw/neue-ki-stimme-karin-kueffmann/": {"text": "Prof. Dr. Karin Küffmann Prof. Dr. Karin Küffmann ist Professorin für Wirtschaftsinformatik an der Westfälischen Hochschule in Gelsenkirchen und Mitglied des Leitungsgremiums der deutschen KI-Initiative für Kommunen »URBAN.KI« zurück »Eingebettete KI, insbesondere im industriellen Umfeld, bietet für die deutsche Wirtschaft noch enormes Potenzial.« Kommunen und Städte stehen in vielen Bereichen vor wachsenden Herausforderungen. Der Fachkräftemangel, fehlende Digitalisierung und wachsende bürokratische Anforderungen sind hierfür nur einige Beispiele. Um diesen Herausforderungen zu begegnen, setzt das Projekt URBAN.KI auf Lösungen der Künstlichen Intelligenz. Gemeinsam mit einem starken Partner- und Unterstützernetzwerk möchte die Initiative eine Vision verwirklichen: eine intelligente Stadt, die durch maßgeschneiderte KI-Anwendungen, beispielsweise in der Energieplanung, der Logistik, dem Straßenbau und der Verkehrssteuerung, zum Leben erweckt wird. Frau Prof. Küffmann, in Ihrer Forschung an der Hochschule Gelsenkirchen beschäftigen Sie sich seit vielen Jahren mit Smart-City-Strategien. Was sind eigentlich Smart Cities? Und was zeichnet diese aus? Smart im Zusammenhang mit Smart Cities würde ich mit intelligenter, nachhaltig orientierter Stadtentwicklung übersetzen wollen. Es sind Städte, die sich an ökologischen, sozialen und wirtschaftlichen Nachhaltigkeitszielen orientieren und mit Informations- und Kommunikationstechnologien (IKT) stetig fortentwickeln. Dazu gehören nun auch verschiedene KI-Technologien, die wir mit URBAN.KI erforschen und nutzbar machen. Die Modellprojekte Smart Cities (MPSC) wie auch etliche aus dem Bitkom- und Hasselhorst-Ranking, aber auch viele ländliche Regionen mit intelligenten lösungsorientierten Einzelprojekten gehörten dazu. Wie hat sich Ihr Forschungsfeld in den letzten Jahren verändert, insbesondere vor dem Hintergrund der immer besser werdenden Generativen KI-Modelle? Die Smart-City-Anwendungen haben sich von der Fokussierung rein digitaler Lösungen zu nachhaltigeren, lösungsorientierten Anwendungen weiterentwickelt. Die Städte fragen heute mehr nach dem Nutzen der digitalen Lösung für bürgerfreundlichere Verwaltungsprozesse oder für nachhaltige Stadtentwicklung. Gerade die Generative KI kann hier hohe Effekte erzielen. Dabei zeigt sich, dass KI-Lösungen in den Kommunen gut geplant, ein- und ausgeführt werden müssen, damit die Verwaltungen und Bürger*innen sie als Helfer und nicht als Feind betrachten. Denn das genau ist das Zeichen der Zeit: Ziel ist es, Menschen insbesondere mit zeitaufwendigen Arbeiten zu unterstützen. Dazu braucht es zeitgemäße und kosteneffiziente IT-Organisationen mit modernen, cloudbasierten Datenarchitekturen. Wo sehen Sie die größten Einsatzchancen von KI in Smart-City-Projekten? Eine riesige Chance ist die vielfältige Nutzungsmöglichkeit von Sprachmodellen, bspw. für die Zusammenfassung von Akten für Bürger*innen und Mitarbeiter*innen, oder auch die Entwicklung von (ein- oder mehrsprachigen) Chat- und Voicebots zur Bürger*inneninformation: Noch zukunftsweisender erscheint mir aber die langfristige Perspektive: die blitzschnelle Zusammenführung von relevanten Daten auf gemeinsamen Datenplattformen zur Einschätzung von Hochwasser-Gefahrenlagen oder zur Mobilitätssteuerung, bspw. im Ruhrgebiet. Diese Projekte müssen langfristig geplant werden, da sie gemeinsame Datenstrukturen erfordern. Gerade mit unserem Ansatz von URBAN.KI haben wir hier Möglichkeiten, mit den smarten Städten gemeinsame Entwicklung von Konzepten und Erprobung von Technologien für diese langfristigen Szenarien zu entwickeln. Sie sind Teil des vierköpfigen Leitungsgremiums der deutschen KI-Initiative für Kommunen URBAN.KI. Erklären Sie uns, was es mit dieser Initiative auf sich hat. Kommunen können durch intensive konzeptionelle und technische Zusammenarbeit viele Wirtschaftlichkeitsreserven in der IT realisieren; Kommunen leiden unter dem Druck von Mehrarbeit, Budgetrestriktionen und der kommenden Pensionierungswelle der geburtenstarken Jahrgänge. Gerade hier macht es Sinn, mit einer bundesweiten Initiative nach gemeinsamen Lösungen zu suchen, die einmal entwickelt, vielfach von Nutzen sein können. URBAN.KI kann über die enge Verbindung zu Kommunen hier auch künftig die Anforderungen erheben und nach dem »einer für alle«-Prinzip effektiv entwickeln. Auch können Kommunen über das aufgebaute Netzwerk von Wissen und Erfahrung der anderen profitieren. In dem Projekt identifizieren Sie sechs Innovationsbereiche, in denen Künstliche Intelligenz eine Rolle spielt. Wie lief die Festlegung dieser Bereiche ab? Die Innovationsfelder entsprechen den typischen Smart-City-Handlungsfeldern: nachhaltige Stadtentwicklung, nachhaltige Mobilität, städtische Klimaresilienz und Klimaschutz, Gebäudeplanung und -steuerung, Sicherheit, Bürgerbeteiligung und Verwaltungseffizienz. Auch kümmern wir uns um die technologischen Basics der sicheren Datenräume und Cybersecurity. Die MPSC-Förderung des Bundesminist", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:58:58.714960+00:00"}, "https://www.ki.nrw/zweite-konferenz-der-ki-nrw-flagship-initiative-bringt-nordrhein-westfaelische-ki-leuchtturmprojekte-nach-bonn/": {"text": "News-Eintrag · KI.NRW-Flagships Zweite Konferenz der KI.NRW-Flagship-Initiative bringt nordrhein-westfälische KI-Leuchtturmprojekte nach Bonn 26.06.2024 Acht Konsortialvorhaben, 47 Projektpartner, ein Ziel: Innovationstreiber »powered by KI.NRW« bündeln Kräfte für projektübergreifende Zusammenarbeit Am 25. Juni trafen sich die Projektleitungen und -teams der mittlerweile acht KI.NRW-Flagship-Projekte zur zweiten KI.NRW-Flagship-Konferenz in Bonn. Auf dem Programm standen Impulsvorträge, interaktive Workshopformate und viel Raum zum gegenseitigen Kennenlernen und Vertiefen bestehender Kontakte. Ziel der Veranstaltung war es, Synergien zu identifizieren und die projektübergreifende Zusammenarbeit zu stärken. »NRW kann KI« – das wurde auch gestern wieder klar, als auf Initiative von KI.NRW sowie des Ministeriums für Wirtschaft, Industrie, Klimaschutz und Energie des Landes Nordrhein-Westfalen MWIKE rund 40 führende KI-Expert*innen aus dem ganzen Bundesland im Bonn Surgical Technology Center (BOSTER) zusammenkamen, um an der zweiten KI.NRW-Flagship-Konferenz teilzunehmen. Für die großen Konsortien, die mit ihren Hauptstandorten in Paderborn, Bielefeld, Siegen, Essen, Düsseldorf, Wuppertal und Sankt Augustin/Bonn angesiedelt sind, bot die Flagship-Konferenz eine zentrale Plattform, um sich auszutauschen, voneinander zu lernen und gemeinsam darüber zu diskutieren, wie der Technologietransfer von der Forschung in die Anwendung gestärkt werden kann. »Mit der Flagship-Initiative unterstützen wir anwendungsbezogene, gesellschaftsrelevante KI-Vorhaben aus unterschiedlichen Bereichen, wie der Medizin, dem Handwerk, der Produktion, dem Handel sowie dem Hochwasserschutz. Dabei schaffen wir Sichtbarkeit für die Aktivitäten der Projekte, verstehen uns aber auch als Impulsgeber und Möglichmacher, um die Fachleute aus den verschiedenen Teams auch untereinander zu vernetzen. Aus unserer Transferarbeit wissen wir: richtig gut wird es, wenn man gemeinsam anpackt. Ich freue mich, dass wir mit unserer zweiten Flagship-Konferenz einen Beitrag leisten konnten, um die vorhandenen Kräfte zu bündeln und eine Basis für den weiteren Austausch zu schaffen«, sagt Dr. Christian Temath, Geschäftsführer bei KI.NRW. Ein interaktives Speeddating gleich zu Beginn der Veranstaltung und eine konzentrierte World Café-Session mit abschließenden Präsentationen aus den Arbeitsgruppen sorgten für den entsprechenden Rahmen. Die Projektteams berichteten außerdem in kurzen Pitches über ihre Projektziele sowie auch explizit über bestehende Herausforderungen, die es auf dem Weg zu den nächsten Meilensteinen zu lösen gilt. Dabei zeigte sich, dass ein projektübergreifender Austausch selbst bei gänzlich unterschiedlichen Zielbranchen fruchtbar sein kann, etwa wenn es um die Aufbereitung und Analyse von Daten geht, die korrekte Kalibrierung von Sensoren, oder die Erstellung von KI-Readiness-Checks, Studien-Designs und Kommunikationsmaterial für Messen und Events. »Dass in den Kaffee-Pausen schon erste Videocalls terminiert wurden, um in den nächsten Tagen noch einmal in kleineren Runden miteinander zu sprechen, bestärkt uns darin, unsere Netzwerkaktivitäten weiter voranzutreiben«, so Temath. Nach der ersten Flagship-Konferenz auf Schloss Birlinghoven im September 2022 und der erfolgreichen zweiten Ausgabe am gestrigen Dienstag wird das Format in Absprache mit den Projektteams auch in den nächsten Jahren wieder ausgerichtet werden. Genauer Informationen zu den einzelnen Projekten finden Sie hier auf der KI.NRW-Website sowie in der neu erschienenen Flagship-Broschüre, in der alle Vorhaben in kompakter Form vorgestellt werden. Download Flagship-Broschüre Impressionen Die Kompetenzplattform Künstliche Intelligenz Nordrhein-Westfalen unterstützt mit der Dachmarke »Flagships powered by KI.NRW« vom Land geförderte Vorhaben als KI-Leuchtturmprojekte. Ziel ist es, einen effizienten Technologietransfer und die enge Zusammenarbeit von Mittelstand, Start-ups, Universitäten, Hochschulen sowie Forschungseinrichtungen in NRW zu gewährleisten und zu unterstützen. KI.NRW-Flagships sind hochinnovative und anwendungsbezogene KI-Projekte in Einzel- und Verbundvorhaben aus unterschiedlichen Branchen, wie Handel, Produktion, Handwerk und Gesundheit. Unter der Schirmherrschaft von KI.NRW werden jene innovationstreibenden Akteure und Kompetenzen vernetzt, die geförderten Vorhaben kommunikativ begleitet und durch die Vermarktung der Ergebnisse der KI-Standort NRW auf europäischer Ebene positioniert. Dabei liegt ein Fokus auf dem nachhaltigen Transfer und der Weiterverwertung der Projektergebnisse. KI.NRW-Flagships ‹ Zurück zur News-Eintrag-Übersicht", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:59:00.963702+00:00"}, "https://www.hlrs.de/de/news/detail/wissenschaftler-des-hlrs-gewinnen-forschungsposter-preis-auf-der-isc26": {"text": "Höchstleistungsrechenzentrum Stuttgart Lösungen Lösungen Rechentypen Höchstleistungsrechnen & Simulation Visualisierung Künstliche Intelligenz Quantencomputing Systeme HPE Cray EX4000 (Hunter) NVIDIA GB200 NVL4 by HPE (HammerHAI) HPE Cray GX5000 (Herder) NEC Cluster (Vulcan) HPE Apollo 6500 Gen10 Plus (Hawk KI-Erweiterung) CAVE HPSS Datenmanagement User Support Betreuung Extended User Support Beratung Software-Bereitstellung Datenmanagement KI Unterstützung Software User-Forschung HLRS-Projekte Training Für die Industrie Öffentlicher Sektor Über Uns Für System-User Wissenschaftler des HLRS gewinnen Forschungsposter-Preis auf der ISC26 Die Forscher untersuchten die Eignung der Cerebras Wafer-Scale Engine zur Beschleunigung herkömmlicher Simulationsabläufe. Daniel Renschler und Jonathan Schäfer, beide wissenschaftliche Mitarbeiter am Höchstleistungsrechenzentrum Stuttgart (HLRS), erhielten auf der ISC High Performance in Hamburg den dritten Preis im Wettbewerb um den „Best Poster Award“. Ihr Poster mit dem Titel SpMV for the Cerebras Wafer-Scale Engine berichtet über die erfolgreiche Implementierung einer SpMV-Methode (Sparse Matrix-Vector Product) auf einem experimentellen, massiv-parallelen Rechenbeschleuniger. Die Wafer-Scale Engine (WSE), hergestellt vom kalifornischen Start-up Cerebras, verfügt über bis zu 900.000 Rechenkern auf einem einzigen, großformatigen Chip. Die einzigartige Architektur des WSE, die für Trainings- und Inferenzanwendungen im Bereich der künstlichen Intelligenz entwickelt wurde, bietet im Vergleich zu herkömmlichen KI-Prozessoren deutlich höhere Geschwindigkeiten. Veröffentlicht am 24. Juni 2026 Kategorien Ankündigungen HLRS-Projekte Future-Computing KI & Datenanalyse Auszeichnungen Alle Nachrichten In der heutigen Generation von Höchstleistungsrechnern werden Prozessoren, die ursprünglich für Anwendungen im Bereich der künstlichen Intelligenz eingesetzt wurden, zunehmend umfunktioniert. Sie beschleunigen herkömmliche Simulationsaufgaben, ermöglichen hybride Arbeitsabläufe, die Simulation und datengesteuerte Methoden kombinieren, und führen datenintensive Aufgaben aus, die auf KI-optimierten Prozessoren schneller bewältigt werden können. Renschler, Schäfer und ihre Kollegen wollten herausfinden, ob sich der WSE ebenfalls auf diese Weise nutzen lässt. Bei ihren Tests konzentrierten sie sich auf SpMV, eine Berechnungsmethode, die häufig in klassischen Simulationsanwendungen wie der Finite-Elemente-Analyse und der numerischen Strömungsmechanik zum Einsatz kommt. Renschler und Schäfer nutzten die Wafer-Scale Engine, um einen hochgradig parallelisierten Bestandteil eines typischen Simulations-Workflows zu beschleunigen, der diese Methode beinhaltet. Außerdem experimentierten sie mit schwacher und starker Skalierung und deckten dabei Engpässe auf, die die Anwendungsleistung beeinträchtigten. So konnte die Forschenden Optimierungsstrategien vorschlagen, mit denen sich die Leistung von SpMV-Methoden auf dem WSE in Zukunft verbessern ließe. Dieses Forschungsprojekt ist Teil der Future-Computing-Gruppe am HLRS unter der Leitung von Dr. Johannes Gebert. Das interdisziplinäre Team testet und bewertet neue Hardwarekonzepte und deren Eignung für typische Höchstleistungsrechneranwendungen. Renschler, Schäfer und Gebert führten dieses Experiment gemeinsam mit Mark Parsons, dem Direktor des EPCC in Edinburgh, durch. — Christopher Williams Höchstleistungsrechenzentrum Stuttgart Nobelstraße 19, 70569 Stuttgart, Deutschland Kontakt & Standort Als Mitglied des Gauss Centre for Supercomputing ist das HLRS eines der drei Bundes­höchst­leistungs­rechen­zentren. www.gauss-centre.eu Das HLRS ist eine zentrale Einrichtung der Universität Stuttgart. www.uni-stuttgart.de © 2026 HLRS. Alle Rechte vorbehalten. Sitemap Impressum Datenschutz Barrierefreiheit Keine Übersetzung vorhanden! × Zu dieser Seite gibt es leider (noch) keine deutsche Übersetzung. Schließen", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:59:02.653553+00:00"}, "https://www.sprind.org/en/actions/challenges/next-frontier-robotics": {"text": "Apply now Your Challenge: Next Frontier Robotics Topic Goal Participate Webinar FAQ Contact Hello, innovators! We need technological breakthroughs to unlock the next S-curve of multi-purpose robotics made in Europe. We need bold approaches and the ambition to move from demos to real-world impact. We need you. Copy link to section: While robots today master repetitive tasks in controlled settings, they still fundamentally struggle with novel tasks, changing conditions or unstructured environments. Widening demographic shifts, a growing labor shortage, and rising competitiveness urgently drive the need for robotics systems that can tackle physical tasks reliably while safeguarding safety and sovereignty. Global competitors in the US and China are investing billions into closed robotics systems, placing Europe at risk of strategic dependence without its own frontier innovations. While Europe retains a world-class manufacturing foundation and research footprint, shaping the next era of robotics demands immediate action to scale next-generation hardware and the intelligent systems to operate them. The SPRIND Challenge Next Frontier Robotics accelerates this leap by backing exceptional teams with moonshot approaches that move beyond incremental optimizations to deliver true capability leaps. Together, we are building a modular, interoperable European robotics ecosystem ready to adapt to the complexity of real-world tasks. Play video The Challenge: Develop full-stack robot platforms that handle complex physical tasks in real environments or novel robotic hardware components with breakthrough capabilities. The challenge covers two complementary paths: Track 1 ‘Integrated Platforms’ challenges teams to orchestrate sub-components into complete multi-purpose robots which can tackle complex, commercial use cases. Track 2 ‘Hardware Components’ focuses on novel hardware such as gripping systems, sensors, or joints that achieve a radical performance leap over the state of the art. Both tracks start by separately testing their approaches in lab environments (Stage 1) prior to moving into the real world (Stage 2), then focus on integration and interoperability into one system (Stage 3) before working on interoperability between different systems (Stage 4). As the challenge progresses, teams are expected to collaborate with complementary challenge participants or external partners to foster the development of standards. This sets the foundation for an interoperable ecosystem, enabling teams (and later customers) to tackle their selected use cases with the best-suited hardware and models under actual operating conditions, moving robotics from the lab into real-world scenarios. The primary focus is on terrestrial, physically interactive applications in industrial and societal settings such as logistics, care support and construction. We support: Potential breakthrough innovations Copy link to section: The challenge runs for 36 months across four sequential stages. A jury of recognized experts supports SPRIND in selecting and evaluating participating teams. SPRIND provides financial backing as an individual fixed-price contract based on the team's cost estimate, offering up to €7,200,000 per team. Beyond funding, SPRIND provides continuous venture development support, coaching, and direct access to new collaboration partners, first customers, and potential investors. Where significant potential for breakthrough innovation is identified, SPRIND may continue providing financial support even after the challenge ends. SPRIND’s mandate is to support technology transfer. Therefore, teams shall aim to incorporate a company, ideally before the challenge starts and show commitment to build an eventually self-sustaining business. The Competition Copy link to section: Detailed information can be found in the call for submission and the participation agreement. Call for Submissions & Participation Agreement Application Copy link to section: The application period runs until October 22, 2026, at 18:00 (CEST). All applications submitted by this deadline will be considered. Applicants applying to both tracks must submit a separate application form for each. This ensures an independent review process, as candidates may advance in one track without advancing in the other. APPLY NOW Webinar Copy link to section: Learn more about the SPRIND Challenge and get your questions answered during our upcoming webinar on October 1. Follow our LinkedIn channel for regular updates. Register here FAQ Copy link to section: WHAT’S THE TIMELINE? Who can apply? WHAT IS MEANT BY \"INTEGRATED PLATFORM\" IN TRACK 1? FOR THE INTEGRATED PLATFORMS TRACK, HOW ADVANCED DOES OUR BASE HARDWARE SYSTEM HAVE TO BE TO BE ELIGIBLE TO APPLY? ARE ALL USE CASES FOR TRACK 1 ELIGIBLE? CAN WE APPLY AS A PURE AI OR SOFTWARE TEAM? CAN I PARTICIPATE IN BOTH TRACKS? TO WHAT EXTENT CAN I USE PARTS FROM OUTSIDE EUROPE? WHAT IF I CANNOT COOPERATE WITH A TEAM FROM THE OTHER TRACK IN STAGE 3 a", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:59:05.134215+00:00"}, "https://www.sprind.org/en/actions/challenges/recoding-medicine": {"text": "Apply now Your Challenge: Recoding Medicine Topic Goal Participate Webinar FAQ Contact Hello, innovators! We need breakthrough innovations to unlock Europe's fragmented health data for AI and to prove its value through concrete medical applications. We need bold teams that turn untapped datasets into groundbreaking innovations for diagnostics, biomarkers, and therapies. We need you. Copy link to section: European hospitals, biobanks, and research institutions hold health data of exceptional depth — imaging archives, longitudinal cohort studies, multi-omics repositories, clinical registries. The raw material for AI-driven breakthroughs in medicine is already here. It remains largely untapped. The central obstacles are easy access, curation, and the lack of structured collaboration between data holders and AI talent. Researchers with the right methods do not have the right data; institutions with valuable datasets lack the capacity and the incentive to make them available. Existing initiatives close only part of the gap. What is missing is an instrument that makes the creation and curation of high-quality, low-barrier health datasets, AI tools, and benchmarks its central goal — and demonstrates their economic value. This is where Recoding Medicine comes in. Play video The Challenge: Curate a unique, high-value health dataset — and prove its worth through a concrete AI application with real clinical or commercial value. Recoding Medicine unlocks new health data for AI innovation and proves its value through concrete applications. Each team curates at least one unique health dataset of high clinical or societal relevance, in line with applicable law. Building on it, each team demonstrates translational or commercial value through at least one concrete AI application — from biomarkers to diagnostics and clinical decision support to drug repurposing. A substantial, documented public contribution is expected from each team; its form, layer, and access model are project-specific. The responsible handling of sensitive health data and the safeguarding of data protection and data sovereignty are guiding principles for every project. We support: Potential breakthrough innovations Copy link to section: The challenge runs over three stages and 24 months. A jury of internationally recognized experts supports SPRIND in the selection. At a Pitch Day in early November 2026, the jury decides which of up to 13 teams start in Stage 1 (12 months); up to 7 continue to Stage 2 and up to 4 to Stage 3 (six months each). SPRIND provides up to €480,000 per team in Stage 1, up to €455,000 in Stage 2, and up to €550,000 in Stage 3 (in each case plus VAT, where applicable). Funding is awarded as pre-commercial procurement (PCP). The Challenge is funded by the Federal Ministry of Health (BMG). Beyond funding, SPRIND supports teams throughout with technical, regulatory, and commercial coaching, opens access to cooperation partners and experts, and organizes Demo Days and match sessions with investors and industry partners. Where breakthrough-innovation potential is evident, further support after the challenge is possible. The Competition Copy link to section: Detailed information can be found in the call for submission and the participation agreement. Call for Submissions & Participation Agreement Application Copy link to section: The application period runs until October 16, 2026, at 18:00 (CET). A prerequisite for participation is demonstrable access to at least one suitable dataset. All applications submitted by this deadline will be considered. APPLY NOW Webinar FAQ Copy link to section: Who can apply? Do I need a dataset to apply? How are teams selected? What kind of dataset is in scope? What can the funding be used for? What public contribution is expected? How is intellectual property handled? What happens during the challenge? Can SPRIND support teams after the challenge? How does SPRIND handle confidentiality? Do you have any questions about the challenge? Write to us at challenge@sprind.org . More Challenges and Funken Broad-Spectrum Antivirals Challenge complete The goal of the Challenge was to expand the repertoire of antiviral therapeutics with breakthrough technologies so that new treatment options will be available in the future and patients can be helped quickly. View challenge Tech Metal Transformation 1st stage of the Challenge We are looking for innovative processes to recover critical metals from complex waste streams such as electronic waste, batteries, or electronic components. During the Challenge, an integrated prototype system is to be developed. View challenge", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:59:07.056249+00:00"}, "https://www.sprind.org/en/actions/challenges/orbital-bioworks": {"text": "1st stage of the Challenge Your Challenge: Orbital Bioworks Topic Goal Teams Jury FAQ Contact Hello, innovators! We need breakthrough innovations to unlock the potential of microgravity for drug development and biomanufacturing. We need bold approaches and the will to put them into orbit. We need you. Copy link to section: Microgravity fundamentally alters fluid dynamics, crystal growth, and biological processes in ways that enable a qualitatively higher level of production – purer protein crystals, stem cells free from mechanical stress, more realistic disease models. On Earth, however, microgravity can be reproduced neither continuously nor at industrial scale. That is precisely why pharma and biotech are the driving forces behind an emerging in-space manufacturing market. The first commercial steps in this market are so far being taken primarily in the US; a European counterweight is missing. Whoever develops the first economically viable applications under microgravity in the coming years will shape the standards for a market that is only just emerging. At stake is Europe's technological and industrial sovereignty in orbital production. Play video Develop and validate the commercially viable use cases in orbital biotechnology with a clear, measurable microgravity advantage. The challenge defines three focus areas in which microgravity promises a scientifically robust advantage and established European platforms exist: cell-free peptide manufacturing, differentiation of stem cells, and organ-on-a-chip for drug testing. We are open to other approaches beyond these focus areas, as long as microgravity offers a demonstrable advantage in your specific use case. We support: Potential breakthrough innovations Copy link to section: The challenge runs over a total period of up to roughly 18 months across two stages. A jury of recognized experts supports SPRIND in the selection. In Stage 1 (four months), up to twelve teams are admitted; at the end, the jury selects up to four teams for a possible Stage 2 (roughly 14 months). Across the two stages, SPRIND provides up to €1,650,000 per team – up to €250,000 in Stage 1 and up to €1,400,000 in the possible Stage 2 (in each case plus VAT, where applicable). Beyond the funding, SPRIND organizes and finances access to microgravity platforms for all teams in Stage 2: a parabolic flight campaign for validation, as well as an orbital mission to the LEO station VAST Haven-1. Through our partner Yuri, a flight-qualified, standardized experimentation platform is available, including technical and life-science coaching. Teams also receive continuous support, easier access to new collaboration partners and experts, and possibly further financial backing where SPRIND sees breakthrough-innovation potential. Teams OSTEORBIT Learn more Hairy Therapeutics Learn more MicroAge Learn more Project DisORBIT Learn more BioOrbit Learn more OrganoTherapeutics Learn more Prosemble Learn more AEGIS Learn more TrueCardium Orbital Learn more VitrofluidiX Learn more OMEGA Learn more INVITRIS Learn more Prof. Dr. Pascale Ehrenfreund Learn more Dr. Jack J. W. A. van Loon Learn more Dr. Isabel Zhang Learn more Prof. Dr. med. Alexander Choukér Learn more Dr. rer. nat. Ruth Hemmersbach Learn more Matthias Wachter Learn more Sigrid Koeth Learn more Matthias Spott Learn more FAQ Copy link to section: Who can apply? Is there a minimum team size? How are teams selected? What can the funding be used for? Who covers the costs for the flights? How is intellectual property regulated? Can SPRIND provide further support after the challenge? Where can I find more information about the technical setup of YURI? Who owns the intellectual property (IP) I share with YURI? Do you have any questions about the challenge? Write to us at challenge@sprind.org . More about this topic Magazine Articles 7/30/2026 EUROPE MUST RETHINK SPACE EXPLORATION From publicly funded space exploration to the Orbital Economy. A strategy for sovereignty, prosperity and security. Read More 9/16/2026 GOVERNMENT AS PARTNER For founders building the technologies Europe depends on, this is the moment to start treating it as a strategic partner. HV and SPRIND have built a playbook to help founders do exactly that. Read More More Challenges and Funken Anti-Drone Response Funke completed We are looking for a fully autonomous system that can detect, classify, and neutralize small and micro UAVs in real time – without weapons, external communication, or human intervention. View challenge Continuous Hormone Monitoring 1st stage of the Challenge We are looking for breakthroughs in continuous hormone monitoring in women's health through next-generation biosensors. View challenge", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:59:09.271678+00:00"}, "https://www.sprind.org/en/actions/challenges/continuous-hormone-monitoring": {"text": "1st stage of the Challenge Your Challenge: Continuous Hormone Monitoring Topic Goal Teams Jury FAQ Contact Hello people with radical new ideas! We need breakthrough innovations to measure hormones. Continuously. Precisely. Personalized. We need bold ideas. We need you. Copy link to section: Hormones control nearly every process in the human body – from metabolism and immune function to cognition and reproduction. Yet how we measure them is static: one moment, one value. Hormones follow highly dynamic patterns however – they fluctuate throughout the day, across the cycle, in response to stress, sleep and nutrition. This dynamic currently remains invisible. Women are particularly affected. Their hormonal systems show the greatest variability – and have been structurally underrepresented in research for a long time. Even today, diagnoses are based on reference ranges that consider neither cycle phase nor time of day nor individual baseline. Diseases such as endometriosis, PCOS (now PMOS), autoimmune disorders or hormone-dependent cardiovascular diseases are detected too late or not understood at all. AI models are trained on the same incomplete data. What we need are sensors that measure continuously in everyday life. Sensors that make visible what remains hidden today. Play video The challenge: to develop biosensors that continuously measure at least four hormones over seven days – with sufficient temporal resolution to capture biological dynamics without loss of information. In the Challenge, teams will develop sensor technologies that enable continuous hormone monitoring for the first time. At least four hormones must be captured – including estrogen, progesterone, LH, FSH, cortisol, testosterone or thyroid hormones. The temporal resolution of measurements must be chosen to capture the specific biological dynamics and pulsatility of the selected hormone panel without loss of information. Measurement occurs over at least seven consecutive days, directly in a biological matrix such as interstitial fluid, sweat or saliva. Each team defines a concrete, transformative use case based on clinical-grade data: Early detection of preeclampsia through longitudinal estrogen profiles? Optimization of hormone-dependent drug dosing across the cycle? Prediction of luteal insufficiency before clinical symptoms emerge? The goal is to develop a platform technology fundamentally capable of making visible highly dynamic processes that remain hidden in current clinical practice due to insufficient data point density. This should create new possibilities for therapy and diagnostics that would not be possible without the developed technology. In parallel, a shared reference data pool is created – the first standardized collection of continuous hormone data across populations, life phases and health conditions. This pool will form the foundation for precise calibration, further research, and AI-powered precision medicine. We support: Potential breakthrough innovations Copy link to section: Teams participating in this Challenge are fully challenged. SPRIND therefore provides intensive and individual support. This includes funding the teams with up to €1.5 million in Stage 1, up to €2 million in Stage 2 and up to €2.5 million in the final Stage 3 of the Challenge. In order to help the teams develop their full potential, SPRIND provides them not only with financial support but also with mentors who accompany, advise and network the work of each team. To enable the teams to concentrate fully on their innovations, we provide funding quickly and unbureaucratically. The first stage starts in July 2026: After one year, the jury decides on the basis of interim evaluations which teams will continue to participate in the Challenge. As finalists, these teams are given the opportunity to drive their project forward for another twelve months. The best teams receive another twelve months in Stage 3 to comprehensively demonstrate their breakthrough. Thinking one step further: Ideas with the potential for a breakthrough innovation must be brought to market to benefit us all – promising projects in this sense can therefore continue to be supported by SPRIND after the Challenge has ended. Teams MoleSense Learn more Non-invasive Continuous Endocrine Phenotyping Learn more Level Zero Health Continuous Hormone Monitoring Learn more Kāhu SiliconBio Learn more Biomarq Health Learn more Eve: Continuous Hormone Monitoring for Fertility Learn more Impli Learn more Phoebe: Photonic Continuous Hormone Biosensor Learn more Jury Dr. Natalia Belokoneva Learn more Dr. Giulia Paggiola Learn more Dr. Michael Schoemaker Learn more Prof. Eugene Oteng-Ntim Learn more Prof. Dr. Susanna Hofmann Learn more Rowan Gardner Learn more Prof. Sheng Xu Learn more Dr. Saurabh Tak Learn more Webinar FAQ Copy link to section: WHICH FINANCIAL SUPPORT WILL I RECEIVE FROM SPRIND? WHICH COSTS CAN BE COVERED BY THE FUNDING? HOW ARE THE INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY RIGHTS REGULATED? CAN INNOVAT", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:59:13.504758+00:00"}, "https://www.sprind.org/en/actions/challenges/next-frontier-ai": {"text": "1st stage of the Challenge Your Challenge: Next Frontier AI Topic Goal Teams Jury FAQ Contact We are looking for the leap to the next S-curve of artificial intelligence. Disruptive approaches with the potential to fundamentally surpass the capabilities of today's systems. We need bold teams. We need you. Copy link to section: Europe's competitiveness in AI innovation remains far behind that of the USA and China. While OpenAI, Anthropic, and DeepSeek invest billions in foundation models, Europe lacks the industrial backbone to independently develop, operate, and scale next-generation AI systems. The consequence: strategic dependency in key sectors – industry, healthcare, public administration, security. The SPRIND Challenge Next Frontier AI aims to change that: a structured, three-stage innovation competition over 24 months that bridges the gap between cutting-edge research and commercialisation. The goal is not the incremental development of existing models – we are looking for creative approaches that push the algorithmic boundaries of today and have the potential to far surpass the expected evolution of existing AI approaches. The Challenge identifies approaches that, with sufficient funding, can lead to genuine breakthroughs, while simultaneously ensuring the development of talented teams with high-performance processes. The result: not just technologically relevant artefacts, but commercial entities with research, deployment, and operational excellence – the seeds of European Frontier AI Labs. Play video The challenge: to transform conceptual frontier approaches into European AI Labs with validated technologies – within 24 months. The Challenge pursues a technology- and application-agnostic approach, deliberately excluding incremental optimisations of existing architectures. We are looking for disruptive model architectures, novel training paradigms, innovative modalities, or systemic multi-agent approaches – with a clear go-to-market strategy and operational excellence. The strategic goal: three European Frontier AI Labs that, following successful scale-up financing, operate competitively on the international stage – technologically, operationally, and financially. We support: Potential breakthrough innovations Copy link to section: The Challenge spans 24 months across three stages. A jury of internationally recognised experts – including Frontier Lab veterans, leading researchers, and deep-tech investors – assists SPRIND in evaluating and selecting teams. Teams participating in this Challenge will be pushed to their full potential. SPRIND provides individualised support at every stage, including financial backing, direct access to a network of experts and potential collaborators, and a coaching and company-building programme. After each stage, the jury reviews results and evaluates which teams demonstrate the greatest breakthrough innovation potential. For Stage 1, up to ten teams can each receive up to €3 million (plus VAT). Up to six teams advance to Stage 2, with funding of up to €8 million (plus VAT) per team. Up to three champions reach Stage 3, with up to €15.5 million (plus VAT) per team. Information about our advocates and partner organisations can be found on the NFAI Initiative Website . Teams Aionic Labs Learn more UMA Learn more Weight Space Labs Learn more Continuous Intelligence Learn more Paradigma Learn more After Labs Learn more Feyer Learn more Ontic Labs Learn more Team in stealth mode Learn more Team in stealth mode Learn more Jury Pim de Witte Co-founder and CEO, General Intuition Learn more Søren Hauberg Professor, Technical University of Denmark Learn more Neil Lawrence DeepMind Professor of Machine Learning, University of Cambridge Learn more Allison Duettmann President & CEO, Foresight Institute Learn more Johannes Otterbach Investment Advisor AI & Quantum, SPRIND Learn more Philipp Herzig Chief Technology Officer, SAP SE Learn more Daniel W. Dippold Founder & CEO, EWOR Learn more Markus Wulfmeier Chief Scientist, Nomagic Learn more Yu-Ting Kuo Honorary Fellow, Hughes Hall, University of Cambridge Learn more Andreas Köpf AI researcher and engineer, Open-Thought Learn more FAQ Copy link to section: What’s the timeline? How does the €125M challenge work? What are the key milestones and deliverables at each stage? How does the jury evaluate applications? What if there's a potential conflict of interest with a jury member? What makes a strong application? What do applicants need to submit? How are submissions evaluated? What technical directions are in scope? What can you expect? Who can apply? What background are you looking for? Do I need a specific legal form? Which teams are eligible to apply? Do we have to buy compute ourselves, or do you provide it? We have little / no entrepreneurial experience. Can you help? Can I combine your funding with other (private or public) funding? How agile and iterative is Next Frontier AI? What should I know about SPRIND’s IP provisions? Do you", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:59:15.668730+00:00"}, "https://www.sprind.org/en/actions/challenges/tech-metal": {"text": "1st stage of the Challenge Your Challenge: Tech Metal Transformation Topic Goal Teams Jury FAQ Contact Hello, innovators! We need breakthrough innovations to develop processes for recovering critical metals from complex waste streams. We need bold ideas. We need you. Copy link to section: Critical metals are indispensable for many aspects of modern life. The potential range of applications for these raw materials and metals in particular is enormous: from high-tech applications to renewable energy technologies, aerospace, electronics and modern manufacturing. Access to many of these critical raw materials is limited to a few producing countries. Despite the central role of these raw materials in industrial production processes as well as their concentrated supply, the recycling rate for critical raw materials is alarmingly low. The aim of the Tech Metal Transformation Challenge is to develop and validate innovative processes for recovering critical metals from complex waste streams such as electronic waste, batteries, electronic components, or other well-justified starting material. Biological, chemical and physical-mechanical approaches are to be combined in a modular system to gain access of these critical metals. The aim is to not only to recover individual metals, the Tech Metal Transformation Challenge is driving forward the development of an end-to-end process in which recovered metals can be directly reused as functional materials with a life-after-next in mind. Play video The task: to develop a functional end-to-end prototype that can process the metal inventory of at least two significantly different EoL products into functional metal. The Challenge addresses a radical change in the current logic of metal recycling: away from classic primary paths such as high-temperature melting processes or unspecific leaching processes - towards highly selective, circular processes with minimal use of energy and chemicals. Novel separation and conversion mechanisms based on biological or molecular recognition could in future also make mixed or finely dispersed metals accessible that were previously considered technically or economically non-recyclable. The potential for a broad industrial transformation is there. We support: Potential breakthrough innovations Copy link to section: The SPRIND Challenge spans three years over three stages. A jury of distinguished experts assists SPRIND in evaluating the applications and chose up eight teams to participate in Stage 1 in December 2025. SPRIND provides substantial, tailored support throughout each phase of the challenge, including financial backing and direct access to a network of experts and potential collaborators. For Stage 1, teams may receive up to EUR 1,500,000 (plus VAT), with funding based on each team’s projected costs. After each stage, the jury will review interim results, evaluating which teams display the strongest potential for transformative innovation. Up to six teams will advance to Stage 2, with funding of up to EUR 2,000,000 (plus VAT) per team. For up to four teams moving into Stage 3, SPRIND will provide up to EUR 2,500,000 (plus VAT) to support further development and implementation. Teams WEEEfficient Learn more Dust to Metals Learn more BIOPEPLEACH Learn more CMETRE Learn more E-Murgy Learn more LanthaCycle Learn more BioXtract Learn more STRATEGIC Learn more Jury Tech Metal Innovators' Day and webinar Copy link to section: On September 10, 2025, SPRIND’s Tech Metal Transformation Challenge held its Innovator’s Day – a vibrant exchange of ideas, shaped by the inspiring contributions of all participants. Here you can find the slides presented at the event. Webinar FAQ Copy link to section: How are intellectual property rights regulated? Is there a minimum team size? Can project consortia also apply? Who decides on my application? What is the timeline of the Challenge? Can funds also be spent after the end of the Challenge? What costs can be covered by the funding? What kind of waste materials should be processed in the Challenge? What are “functional products” in the context of the Challenge? Do you have any questions about the Challenge? Write to us at challenge@sprind.org . More about this topic Magazine Articles 4/20/2026 SPRIND CHALLENGE ON CRITICAL METALS SPRIND intends to establish a new recycling industry – now the National Science Foundation is getting involved. A conversation about speed, technology and transatlantic cooperation with SPRIND Innovation Manager Patrick Rose. Read More 7/30/2025 Transatlantic partnership between SPRIND and NSF Reaches new milestone SPRIND launches Tech Metal Transformation Challenge in Munich. Read More 11/18/2025 NSF starts Tech Metal Transformation Challenge Challenge launched in the United States based on the SPRIND model Read More More Challenges and Funken Recoding Medicine Apply now We need breakthrough innovations to unlock Europe's fragmented health data for AI and to prove its value through conc", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:59:18.228283+00:00"}, "https://www.sprind.org/en/actions/challenges/composite-learning": {"text": "2nd stage of the Challenge Your Challenge: Composite Learning Topic Goal Teams Jury FAQ Contact Hello, innovators! We need breakthrough innovations to train AI models across decentralized and heterogenous nodes. We need bold ideas. We need you. Copy link to section: AI is revolutionizing industries like healthcare, finance, and automotive, but only the biggest players have the resources to develop the most powerful models. High-performance training still depends on costly, centralized data centers, limiting access for SMEs and startups—especially in Germany and Europe, where advanced computing resources lag behind. Composite Learning combines distributed, decentralized and federated learning, offering a new approach to AI and allowing models to be trained across diverse systems without the need of centralized data centers. This method lets organizations collaborate and train models securely, preserving data privacy and making cutting-edge AI accessible to more companies. However, new solutions are needed to overcome the limitations of today’s systems, such as a lack of compatibility between different devices, communication bottlenecks, and reliance on central update servers. Play video The task: Your mission is to create a robust, scalable framework for Composite Learning that can handle AI training across diverse, decentralized systems. The focus is on developing solutions that enable efficient model training on heterogeneous Hardware, from high-performance GPUs to CPUs of different types and manufacturers. Solutions must also be resilient, dynamically adapting to computing resource fluctuations and device outages. The teams will deliver a functional core for this framework as open source, which will serve as the foundation for further development, including commercial services and proprietary product features. We support: Potential breakthrough innovations Copy link to section: The SPRIND Challenge spans 30 months over three stages. A jury of distinguished experts will assist SPRIND in evaluating the applications and choosing up to ten teams to participate in Stage 1. SPRIND provides substantial, tailored support throughout each phase of the challenge, including financial backing and direct access to a network of experts and potential collaborators. For Stage 1, teams may receive up to EUR 530,000 (plus VAT), with funding based on each team’s projected costs. After each stage, the jury will review interim results, evaluating which teams display the strongest potential for transformative innovation. Up to seven teams will advance to Stage 2, with funding of up to EUR 520,000 (plus VAT) per team. For those moving into Stage 3, SPRIND will provide up to EUR 600,000 (plus VAT) to support further development and implementation. Start of the 2nd stage Copy link to section: On January 29 and 30, 2026, the expert jury selected four teams for stage 2. Stage 2 will run until the next jury session at the end of October 2026. Teams SymphonyLearn Learn more Planetary Compute Platform Learn more exalsius Learn more CambridgeFlower Learn more Jury Johannes Otterbach Katharine Jarmul Learn more Dr. Wolfgang Stille Learn more Tian Li Learn more Hans Ramsl Learn more Gerhard Wunder Learn more Andreas Unseld Learn more Marc Alexander Kühn Learn more FAQ Copy link to section: Can the funds also be spent after the end of the Challenge? What costs can be covered by the funding? How is the evaluation carried out? How are intellectual property rights regulated? Is there a minimium size for teams? Can project consortia apply for the SPRIND Challenge, too? Who decides on my application? Do you have any questions about the Challenge? Write to us at challenge@sprind.org . More about this topic Magazine Articles 2/4/2025 Expanding decentralized AI model training The jury has selected seven teams for the first stage of the SPRIND Challenge Composite Learning . They now have one year to further develop a robust, scalable framework that can handle AI training across diverse, decentralized systems. Read More 3/23/2025 SPRIND Challenge Days Composite Learning Seven teams met at the Berlin Data Space in mid-March to begin the first stage of the SPRIND Challenge Composite Learning with the aim of reshaping the future of artificial intelligence. Read More 5/19/2025 Special challenges require special formats In an interview, challenge officer Jano Costard talks about the competition that transforms ideas into breakthrough innovations. Read More More Challenges and Funken Anti-Drone Response 2.0 2nd stage SPRIND Funke A fully autonomous system is to be developed, capable of neutralizing small and micro UAVs of up to 25 kg and 200 km/h autonomously, precisely, and non-destructively in real time. View challenge Deepfake Detection and Prevention Funke completed The prototype to be developed must demonstrate how deepfakes images can be reliably detected and authenticated. It can be AI-supported and should be able to continuously adapt to new deepf", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:59:20.356822+00:00"}, "https://www.sprind.org/en/actions/challenges/biomanufacturing": {"text": "3rd stage of the Challenge Your Challenge: Circular Biomanufacturing Topic Goal Teams The Jury FAQ Contact Hello people with radical new ideas! We need breakthrough innovations to convert waste streams directly into new products. Local. Environmentally friendly. Sustainable. We need bold ideas. We need you. Copy link to section: Until now, our manufacturing processes have almost entirely been based on the use of newly, mined raw materials with not enough coming from the recycling of waste streams. This places an enormous burden on the environment and our society. In addition, dependencies remain in global supply chains that could be reduced through access to local materials. Instead, we can create a circular economy in which new products are manufactured locally, using valorized waste streams as a source for raw materials, to build more sustainable and resilient production platforms. To achieve this, biomanufacturing processes must be developed to market maturity and directly integrated with modern production processes. Scientific advances in recent years have produced new findings and methods that can significantly increase the performance of biomanufacturing processes and open up new application possibilities. Although alternative ways of producing a wide range of products to replace the conventional petrochemical or chemical manufacturing processes have gone to market, breakthroughs have so far only been achieved in niche applications. We need to reach the goal where the majority of bulk products are made through biomanufacturing processes that enable the use of locally available raw materials. Play video The challenge: to develop an end-to-end prototype that processes various carbonaceous waste streams into new products as a continuous bioproduction process. The prototype must demonstrate how carbonaceous waste streams can be processed and fed to microbes as food. The overall bioproduction process shall not use E. Coli or Saccharomyces cerevisiae and shall demonstrate continuous production over a period of at least 180 days during the Challenge. At the end of the process, at least three different products should be produced using a modern manufacturing process, such as additive manufacturing. We support: Breakthrough innovations Copy link to section: The Challenge started in November 2023 and runs over a three-year period. A panel of globally recognized experts assisted SPRIND in evaluating the applications and selected the participating teams. During the Challenge period, teams further develop their bioproduction technology to achieve the Challenge goal. Teams participating in this Challenge are fully challenged. SPRIND therefore provides intensive and individual support. In order to unleash the full potential, SPRIND provides a coach to accompany each team's work, advise them and network them. After one year and after two years, the jury reconvened in each case to evaluate the interim status and decided which approaches have the greatest breakthrough innovation potential and which teams can prove themselves in the Challenge until the end. Start of the 3rd stage Copy link to section: In October 2025, the expert jury selected the participants for the third stage of the Circular Biomanufacturing Challenge. Over the next twelve months, five teams will each receive up to €2.5 million to further develop their technology. The teams will also be supported and advised by SPRIND and connected with other experts and coaches. At the end of the year, a decision will be made as to which teams will win the challenge. The Teams Amphistar Whether in shampoo, toothpaste, or printer ink, surfactants are everywhere. Most surfactants are made from fossil fuels or tropical raw materials such as palm oil. Amphistar develops sustainable surfactants that are derived from waste. Learn more BioTreasure BioTreasure uses bacteria and yeast to produce ingredients for cosmetics and food. Its mission is to replace oil-based processes used for mass products with biotechnological methods. Learn more Biophelion Unwelcome in the bathroom and on wallpaper at home, it has been a star in laboratories for decades: the mould. Biophleion turns waste into polyester – the yeast-like fungus is designed to convert various industrial by-products into usable chemicals. Learn more C3 Biotech With the help of a bacterium, C3Biotech converts various agricultural waste streams into acrylic glass. The resulting materials can be used to manufacture new products, such as toys. Learn more EveryCarbon EveryCarbon aims to combine traditional waste disposal and productive biotechnology in a single process. The team recycles waste streams from household and industrial waste and uses them to produce new materials. Learn more Science Youtuber Jacob Beautemps introduces the Challenge teams at Breaking Lab Die Jury Copy link to section: FAQ Copy link to section: Which waste streams can I use to achieve the Challenge goal? What biotechnological processes can I use ", "ok": true, "fetched": "2026-09-22T07:59:22.576212+00:00"}}